WEBVTT

00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:03.820
<v Ira Glass>From WBEZ Chicago, it's This American Life. I'm Ira Glass.
<v Ira Glass>来自芝加哥WBEZ，这里是《美国生活》。我是艾拉·格拉斯。

00:00:03.820 --> 00:00:29.210
<v Ira Glass>There's this theater company in Chicago that, for years, put out a new show every single week where they performed 30 plays in 60 minutes, called the Neo-Futurists. And when we first broadcast today's program all the way back in 2003, a bunch of us were sitting around talking about that group. And one of us had just seen one of their shows. And we thought, that would be really fun-- 30 stories in 60 minutes? Let's try that.
<v Ira Glass>芝加哥有一家剧团，多年来每周都会上演一场全新的演出，在60分钟内表演30出戏，他们叫“新未来主义者”。当我们2003年第一次播出今天的节目时，我们几个人坐在一起聊过这个团体。其中一个人刚看了他们的演出，我们当时想，那一定很有趣——60分钟讲30个故事？我们也来试试吧。

00:00:29.210 --> 00:00:45.060
<v Ira Glass>Instead of our usual-- each week we choose a theme, bring you three or four stories, blah, blah, blah, public radio, very reflective, what kinds of stories would we end up with if we did 30 in one hour? Or even 20? What would it sound like?
<v Ira Glass>不同于我们平时的模式——每周选择一个主题，带来三四个故事，巴拉巴拉，公共广播，非常反思性——如果我们一小时讲30个故事，最后会是什么样？甚至20个？听起来会是什么样？

00:00:45.060 --> 00:00:57.220
<v Ira Glass>We honestly had no idea. And so, that's what we're going to do today. Today we're going to find out. And I have to say, time is wasting if we're going to fit everything in.
<v Ira Glass>老实说我们完全没概念。所以，这就是我们今天要做的。今天我们要一探究竟。我得说，如果要塞进这么多内容，时间可耽误不得。

00:00:57.220 --> 00:01:05.770
<v Ira Glass>How many will it be? How many will we squeeze in? There is but one way to find out, and that is to stick around.
<v Ira Glass>会有多少个？我们能塞进多少个？只有一个办法知道，那就是继续听下去。

00:01:05.770 --> 00:01:38.030
<v Ira Glass>(HOST) IRA GLASS: And let's begin with this one, Act One-- Don't I Know You? Throughout this hour we're going to be bringing you two-minute documentaries, fantastically short works of fiction, and all kinds of little stories that we ordinarily can't use on the radio show because they are just too short, even though they're really fun stories to listen to and perfect stories for the radio. Our first story is one like that. It's one of those stories-- you know how people you know have their greatest hits stories of things that happened to them? We went on a massive search for stories like that from all over the country, and this is one of the stories like that that we found.
<v Ira Glass>第一幕——我不认识你吗？在接下来的这一小时里，我们将为大家带来两分钟的纪录片、极短的虚构作品，以及各种各样的短篇小故事，因为它们太短了，平时没法在节目里播，但它们听起来非常有趣，也非常适合电台播放。我们的第一个故事就是这类。你知道人们常有关于自己经历的“最佳精选”故事吗？我们在全国范围内进行了大规模搜寻，这就是我们找到的故事之一。

00:01:38.030 --> 00:01:47.970
<v Ira Glass>It happened to this actor named Tate Donovan. He told it to our producer Starlee Kine. He was out one night going to a Broadway play with a friend, being treated in this way he never gets treated.
<v Ira Glass>这是演员泰特·多诺万的亲身经历。他告诉了我们的制片人斯塔莉·凯恩。那天晚上他和朋友去看百老汇演出，受到了他从未享受过的特殊待遇。

00:01:47.970 --> 00:02:00.040
<v Tate Donovan>We're sitting around waiting for it to start. And I'm not a very-- I'm not a sort of a recognizable actor. I'm an actor who works, but I never get recognized.
<v Tate Donovan>我们坐着等演出开始。我并不是一个……我不是那种容易被认出来的演员。我是个有戏拍的演员，但我从没被认出来过。

00:02:00.040 --> 00:02:17.470
<v Tate Donovan>So all of a sudden, the 10 minutes we're sitting there for it to start, three or four people come up to me and recognize me. I mean, they know exactly who I am. And they are quoting lines from a television show I was on. And like, hey, you were Joshua on Friends.
<v Tate Donovan>突然，在演出开始前的十分钟里，三四个人走过来认出了我。我的意思是，他们非常清楚我是谁，还引用了我在一部电视剧里的台词。比如，嘿，你是《老友记》里的乔舒亚。

00:02:17.470 --> 00:02:30.600
<v Tate Donovan>And I've always admired stars who were really gracious. So you always think, that's what I want to be like. I want to be really friendly when I'm famous. So I wanted to be friendly and sweet, and go out to the people. They don't have to come to me all the time.
<v Tate Donovan>我一直很钦佩那些非常有礼貌的明星。所以你总是想，我以后也要那样。等我出名了，我要变得非常友好。所以我当时想表现得友好亲切，主动去接触人们。他们不必一直主动来找我。

00:02:30.600 --> 00:02:38.760
<v Starlee Kine>[LAUGHS] So for a little window of time, though, you were exactly the kind of celebrity that you wanted to always be. You were gracious and reserved.
<v Starlee Kine>[笑] 但在那个短暂的瞬间，你表现得正是你一直想成为的那种名流。你既优雅又矜持。

00:02:38.760 --> 00:02:49.080
<v Tate Donovan>Yeah, and warm, you know what I mean? I wasn't like one of these distant celebrities. I was like, hey, I was genuine. They all left thinking, that guy's a really great guy. He's so sweet.
<v Tate Donovan>是啊，而且很热情，你懂我的意思吗？我不想表现得像那种高高在上的名流。我就想，嘿，我很真诚。他们离开时一定觉得，那家伙真是个好人，他太贴心了。

00:02:49.080 --> 00:02:53.020
<v Tate Donovan>I was exactly how I wanted to be. I was doing it. I was doing great.
<v Tate Donovan>我表现得正是我想要的样子。我做到了，我做得很好。

00:02:53.020 --> 00:03:14.250
<v Tate Donovan>And then the kid with the camera came along. This nervous kid-- I don't know, he must have been 16 years old. He's in a rented tuxedo, unbelievably shy and awkward, and he's got acne. And he's got a camera in his hand.
<v Tate Donovan>然后那个带着相机的孩子出现了。那个紧张的孩子——我不知道，他肯定才16岁。他穿着租来的燕尾服，羞涩尴尬得难以置信，还满脸痤疮。他手里拿着相机。

00:03:14.250 --> 00:03:32.880
<v Tate Donovan>And underneath the marquee is his date, who is in literally a prom dress, and she's got a corsage. And she's really nervous and sort of clutching her hands. And he sort of comes up to me and he sort of mumbles something about a picture.
<v Tate Donovan>遮阳篷下站着他的女伴，穿着真正的舞会礼服，戴着胸花。她很紧张，双手紧握。他走过来，含糊不清地说着想拍张照片。

00:03:32.880 --> 00:03:43.720
<v Tate Donovan>And I just feel for him. So I'm like, absolutely, my gosh. Sure, no problem. My God, you poor thing. And I go up to his girlfriend and I wrap my arms around her.
<v Tate Donovan>我很同情他，所以我说，当然，天哪。没问题。天啊，你这可怜的孩子。我走上前去，搂住他女朋友的肩膀。

00:03:43.720 --> 00:03:48.660
<v Tate Donovan>And I'm like, hey, where are you from? Fantastic, you going to see the play? That's great.
<v Tate Donovan>我说，嘿，你们从哪来的？太棒了，你们是来看戏的吗？真不错。

00:03:48.660 --> 00:03:56.820
<v Tate Donovan>And the guy is sort of not taking the photograph very quickly. He's just sort of staring at me. And he's got his camera in his hands and it's down by his chin.
<v Tate Donovan>那小伙子并没有马上拍照。他只是盯着我。他手里拿着相机，放在下巴位置。

00:03:56.820 --> 00:04:08.430
<v Tate Donovan>And she's very stiff and awkward. And I don't know what to do, so I just lean across, and I kiss her on the cheek. And I'm like, all right, come on, take the picture. Hurry up.
<v Tate Donovan>女方显得非常僵硬和尴尬。我不知道该怎么办，所以我凑过去，亲了亲她的脸颊。我说，好了，快点，拍照吧。快点。

00:04:08.430 --> 00:04:23.090
<v Tate Donovan>And finally he sort of snaps it. And I'm like, OK, it was really wonderful to meet you. And he just stammered over to me and was like, um, [CLICKS TONGUE] could you take a picture of us?
<v Tate Donovan>最后他拍了下来。我说，好啦，很高兴见到你们。他结结巴巴地对我说了句，呃，[弹舌声] 你能帮我们拍张照吗？

00:04:23.090 --> 00:04:24.580
<v Starlee Kine>[GROANS]
<v Starlee Kine>[呻吟]

00:04:24.580 --> 00:04:33.820
<v Tate Donovan>And the whole time, he just wanted me to take a picture of him and his girlfriend underneath the awning of the play. He didn't want a picture of me. He had no idea who I was. [LAUGHS]
<v Tate Donovan>原来他全程只是想让我帮他和女朋友在戏院遮阳篷下拍张照。他根本不想拍我。他压根不知道我是谁。[笑]

00:04:33.820 --> 00:04:37.630
<v Starlee Kine>[LAUGHS]
<v Starlee Kine>[笑]

00:04:37.630 --> 00:04:57.160
<v Tate Donovan>Oh, God. They were in shock. I don't think they'd ever come across a human being acting this way. I mean, could you imagine-- you ask someone to take a picture, and you just get in it yourself, and kiss them, and hey.
<v Tate Donovan>哦，天哪。他们都惊呆了。我觉得他们从未见过有人会这样。想象一下——你请人帮忙拍照，结果对方直接闯进镜头，还要亲你，还跟你自来熟。

00:04:57.160 --> 00:05:02.510
<v Tate Donovan>[LAUGHTER]
<v Tate Donovan>[笑声]

00:05:02.510 --> 00:05:09.310
<v Ira Glass>Tate Donovan with Starlee Kine.
<v Ira Glass>泰特·多诺万与斯塔莉·凯恩。

00:05:09.310 --> 00:05:28.800
<v Ira Glass>(HOST) IRA GLASS: Act Two-- No, Of Course I Know You. One of the things that's been really interesting about putting this show together this week is going to some of our regular contributors and commissioning stories that are just two or three minutes long from people who normally write stories that are 10 times that length. This story is an example of that, from Scott Carrier in Salt Lake City.
<v Ira Glass>第二幕——不，我当然认识你。这周制作节目的过程中，最有趣的事情之一就是向我们的一些固定撰稿人约稿，邀请他们创作两三分钟的故事，而这些人平时写的稿子通常都是这个篇幅的十倍。这个故事就是一个例子，来自盐湖城的斯科特·卡里尔。

00:05:28.800 --> 00:05:40.610
<v Scott Carrier>Who was that woman, that woman I just saw while walking out of the restaurant? She was sitting at a table by the door, glasses, a hat, like Lois Lane.
<v Scott Carrier>那个女人是谁？我刚才走出餐厅时看到的那个女人。她坐在门边的桌子旁，戴着眼镜，戴着帽子，看起来像露易丝·莱恩。

00:05:40.610 --> 00:05:49.680
<v Scott Carrier>She looked at me like she knew me. And I said hello because I thought I recognized her. But I couldn't remember her name, so I just kept going out the door.
<v Scott Carrier>她看着我的眼神好像认识我。我说了声你好，因为我以为我认出她了。但我记不起她的名字，于是我就直接走出门口了。

00:05:49.680 --> 00:06:01.890
<v Scott Carrier>Now I can't even remember how I know her. I know I know her, or used to know her. Somehow she was very important to me. She helped me out in a time of trouble.
<v Scott Carrier>现在我甚至记不起我是怎么认识她的了。我知道我认识她，或者曾经认识她。不知为何她对我非常重要。我在困难时期她曾帮助过我。

00:06:01.890 --> 00:06:18.040
<v Scott Carrier>She used to roll her eyes. I'd say something dumb and she'd roll her eyes and get me something I needed, even though she didn't have to. Maybe she works in the library, or the county recorder's office, or at the newspaper.
<v Scott Carrier>她以前总是翻白眼。我说什么傻话她就会翻白眼，但还是会给我需要的东西，尽管她没必要这么做。也许她在图书馆、县记录员办公室或者报社工作。

00:06:18.040 --> 00:06:36.900
<v Scott Carrier>I think I may have been in love with her. No, she's too young. I was never in love with her, not in that way. It's just that I wanted something, needed something. And she was able to give it to me, almost out of the goodness of her heart. And now I can't even remember who she is.
<v Scott Carrier>我想我可能曾经爱上过她。不，她太年轻了。我从来没爱上过她，不是那种爱。只是我当时想要什么，需要什么。而她几乎是出于好心给了我。现在我连她是谁都记不起来了。

00:06:36.900 --> 00:06:47.780
<v Scott Carrier>I'm sick. I'm old. I should just walk out into traffic and kill myself. At home, at night, I go to sleep, searching for the lost memory.
<v Scott Carrier>我病了。我老了。我应该直接走到车流中自杀算了。晚上在家睡觉时，我总是搜寻那段丢失的记忆。

00:06:47.780 --> 00:07:10.750
<v Scott Carrier>Did I meet her down by the river in a canoe, or was it on a ferry in southeastern Alaska, or at the Foreign Correspondents Club in Phnom Penh along the Mekong? She has something to do with water, with life, with mud.
<v Scott Carrier>我是在河边的独木舟上认识她的，还是在阿拉斯加东南部的渡轮上，又或是在湄公河畔金边的外国记者俱乐部？她肯定和水有关，和生活有关，和泥土有关。

00:07:10.750 --> 00:07:27.320
<v Scott Carrier>I sleep poorly, turning and maybe even groaning in anguish. I don't care about the woman anymore. I'm worried for myself. I feel as if there's a black hole in my brain, and slowly but surely, it's swallowing all the memories of my life.
<v Scott Carrier>我睡不好，辗转反侧，甚至痛苦地呻吟。我不在乎那个女人了。我担心我自己。我觉得大脑里有个黑洞，正慢慢地、肯定地吞噬着我一生中所有的记忆。

00:07:27.320 --> 00:07:35.140
<v Scott Carrier>I get up at 5:30 and drive to work in the dark. I feel terrible. I look like a piece of gum in the gutter.
<v Scott Carrier>我5点半起床，在黑暗中开车去上班。我感觉糟透了。我看起来像排水沟里的口香糖。

00:07:35.140 --> 00:07:56.990
<v Scott Carrier>I pull in to Java Joe's to get some chemical help, and there she is behind the drive-up window. I want to tell her I love her, but I don't because it would be too weird. All I can say is, wow. And she rolls her eyes and gets me my cup of coffee.
<v Scott Carrier>我开车去Java Joe's寻求化学提神，她就在免下车窗口后面。我想告诉她我爱她，但我没说，因为那太奇怪了。我只能说，哇。她翻了个白眼，递给我那杯咖啡。

00:07:56.990 --> 00:08:10.790
<v Ira Glass>Scott Carrier in Salt Lake City. If you haven't heard his podcast yet, it's called Home of the Brave.
<v Ira Glass>盐湖城的斯科特·卡里尔。如果你还没听过他的播客，名字叫《勇敢之家》。

00:08:10.790 --> 00:08:24.420
<v Ira Glass>(HOST) IRA GLASS: Act Three-- It's Commerce That Brings Us Together. In 1997, Susan Drury and her husband moved to rural Tennessee, not too far from the Alabama line. They grew attached to the local radio station.
<v Ira Glass>第三幕——商业让我们走到一起。1997年，苏珊·德鲁里和丈夫搬到了田纳西州的农村，离阿拉巴马州界线不远。他们对当地的广播电台产生了感情。

00:08:24.420 --> 00:08:42.590
<v Susan Drury>We couldn't really get any other radio stations at the house, but WKSR has a lot going for it-- the way they read the obituaries on the air, the way people call in during the tornado to tell everybody they're OK and where the roads are flooded, the ads from the same downtown stores over and over and over. And then there's the show we like best.
<v Susan Drury>在家里我们收不到别的电台，但WKSR有很多可取之处——他们广播讣告的方式，龙卷风期间人们打进电话告诉大家他们平安无事、哪里的路被淹了，还有那些来自市中心同一家商店一遍又一遍的广告。然后还有我们最喜欢的节目。

00:08:42.590 --> 00:08:48.090
<v Don Estep>Here we go. It's time once again for another edition of Swap and Shop here on WKSR.
<v Don Estep>开始吧。又是WKSR的《交换与购物》节目时间了。

00:08:48.090 --> 00:09:02.000
<v Susan Drury>Swap and Shop is a low-tech, personable sort of Ebay. It's not fancy or particularly well-produced or anything. It's just a show where people call in to say what they want to sell or buy or give away. They give their phone number, and that's it.
<v Susan Drury>《交换与购物》是一个低技术、亲民版的eBay。它不花哨，制作也不精良。它只是一个让人们打电话来说想买、卖或赠送什么的节目。他们报上电话号码，就这么简单。

00:09:02.000 --> 00:09:04.050
<v Don Estep>Swap And Shop, good morning.
<v Don Estep>《交换与购物》，早上好。

00:09:04.050 --> 00:09:07.520
<v Radio Caller>(SUBJECT) RADIO CALLER #1: Good morning, I have a couch for sale I need to advertise.
<v Radio Caller>广播听众 #1：早上好，我有个沙发要卖，想做个广告。

00:09:07.520 --> 00:09:08.600
<v Don Estep>OK.
<v Don Estep>好的。

00:09:08.600 --> 00:09:20.080
<v Radio Caller>(SUBJECT) RADIO CALLER #1: It's a Lane recliner couch. It's six weeks old. It listed for $1,140, and I'll sell it for $600. We've just got one too many.
<v Radio Caller>广播听众 #1：这是Lane牌躺椅沙发。用了六周。原价1140美元，我卖600美元。我们家沙发多了一个。

00:09:20.080 --> 00:09:22.540
<v Don Estep>OK, and your phone number?
<v Don Estep>好的，电话号码是多少？

00:09:22.540 --> 00:09:25.690
<v Radio Caller>(SUBJECT) RADIO CALLER #1: 468-2524.
<v Radio Caller>广播听众 #1：468-2524。

00:09:25.690 --> 00:09:26.810
<v Don Estep>All right, sir, thank you for calling.
<v Don Estep>好的，先生，谢谢你的来电。

00:09:26.810 --> 00:09:28.210
<v Radio Caller>(SUBJECT) RADIO CALLER #1: Thank you. Have a good day.
<v Radio Caller>广播听众 #1：谢谢。祝你愉快。

00:09:28.210 --> 00:09:33.040
<v Don Estep>You too. There's a couch for sale at 468-2524.
<v Don Estep>你也一样。有人要卖沙发，电话468-2524。

00:09:33.040 --> 00:09:50.080
<v Susan Drury>The show is hosted by a guy named Don Estep, and he almost never comments on anything people are trying to buy or sell. He's like a lot of my neighbors, particularly the men. The attitude is, your business is your business. There's no shame in tough times, and nobody turns himself inside out to tell you everything.
<v Susan Drury>节目主持人叫唐·埃斯蒂普，他几乎从不对人们买卖的东西发表意见。他就像我的很多邻居一样，尤其是男人们。态度就是：你的事就是你的事。日子难过也不丢人，没人会把心掏出来告诉你所有细节。

00:09:50.080 --> 00:10:02.990
<v Radio Caller>(SUBJECT) RADIO CALLER #2: Yes, I have for sale a table and chairs, a microwave, and a washer and a bedroom suite. And I also want to buy a car, a small car.
<v Radio Caller>广播听众 #2：是的，我要卖一套桌椅、一台微波炉、一台洗衣机和一套卧室家具。我还想买辆车，小车。

00:10:02.990 --> 00:10:04.860
<v Don Estep>OK, there's a table and chairs.
<v Don Estep>好的，有一套桌椅。

00:10:04.860 --> 00:10:10.360
<v Susan Drury>Mostly you're just left to wonder about the story behind these things. You don't get too many answers.
<v Susan Drury>大多数时候，你只能去猜测这些物品背后的故事。你不会得到太多答案。

00:10:10.360 --> 00:10:12.130
<v Don Estep>Swap and Shop, good morning.
<v Don Estep>《交换与购物》，早上好。

00:10:12.130 --> 00:10:23.580
<v Radio Caller>(SUBJECT) RADIO CALLER #3: Yes, I'd like to buy a used trampoline. It doesn't matter what shape it's in or if it even has a tarp on it or not. And my telephone number is 629--
<v Radio Caller>广播听众 #3：是的，我想买个二手蹦床。不管形状怎么样，有没有篷布都无所谓。我的电话号码是629——

00:10:23.580 --> 00:10:34.090
<v Susan Drury>Swap and Shop is not unique to this station. Local stations across the country have these radio classifieds type shows. WKSR's version has a regular segment called "The Doggone Show."
<v Susan Drury>《交换与购物》并不是这个电台独有的。全国各地的当地电台都有这种广播分类广告节目。WKSR的版本有一个固定环节叫“狗狗秀”。

00:10:34.090 --> 00:10:57.190
<v Don Estep>Folks have found a black, white, and brown young female beagle with a white tip on its tail, and, more than likely, a family pet. If you'll call 565-4505-- is that number. Also four cows have been found. And you think they might belong to you--
<v Don Estep>有人发现了一只黑白棕三色的小雌性比格犬，尾巴尖是白的，很可能是家养宠物。如果你打565-4505——号码就是这个。另外还发现了四头牛。如果你觉得可能是你家的——

00:10:57.190 --> 00:11:12.380
<v Susan Drury>That first winter in our house, we had no heat, which we thought was adventurous, but was, in fact, just cold. And when we heard a lady call in with an ugly but functional wood stove for sale for $75, we called her. We got it, and we were toasty.
<v Susan Drury>搬进新房的第一个冬天，我们没有暖气，我们觉得这很冒险，但实际上只是冷。当我们听到一位女士打电话说要卖一个丑但实用的烧木炉，标价75美元时，我们买了它，然后我们全身就暖和了。

00:11:12.380 --> 00:11:22.150
<v Radio Caller>(SUBJECT) RADIO CALLER #4: Hey, Don, that little black and brown and white beagle with the tip on her tail that we found? Well, we lost her, so you can quit advertising-- on Barnett Road.
<v Radio Caller>广播听众 #4：嘿，唐，我们发现的那只黑棕白相间、尾巴尖带白的小比格犬？我们把它丢了，所以你可以停止广告了——在巴尼特路上。

00:11:22.150 --> 00:11:23.250
<v Don Estep>On the Barnett Road?
<v Don Estep>在巴尼特路上？

00:11:23.250 --> 00:11:23.730
<v Radio Caller>(SUBJECT) RADIO CALLER #4: Yeah.
<v Radio Caller>广播听众 #4：是的。

00:11:23.730 --> 00:11:24.680
<v Don Estep>All right.
<v Don Estep>好的。

00:11:24.680 --> 00:11:25.160
<v Radio Caller>(SUBJECT) RADIO CALLER #4: Thank you.
<v Radio Caller>广播听众 #4：谢谢。

00:11:25.160 --> 00:11:31.120
<v Don Estep>Thank you for calling.
<v Don Estep>谢谢你的来电。

00:11:31.120 --> 00:11:37.210
<v Ira Glass>Susan Drury, Tennessee.
<v Ira Glass>田纳西州的苏珊·德鲁里。

00:11:37.210 --> 00:11:47.900
<v Ira Glass>(HOST) IRA GLASS: Act Four-- The Sound of One Hand Waving. For this one, let's go to the beach for a one-minute and four-second vacation on Nantucket Island.
<v Ira Glass>第四幕——单手挥舞的声音。这次，让我们去海边，在南塔克特岛度过一分零四秒的假期。

00:11:47.900 --> 00:11:50.290
<v Ira Glass>[WAVES CRASHING]
<v Ira Glass>[海浪拍打声]

00:11:50.290 --> 00:12:02.200
<v Patty Martin>When we were in the water and we realized we weren't able to get back in, we had some friends that were on the beach. And so we started waving to them. We were kind of doing this double-hand wave thing over our heads.
<v Patty Martin>我们在水里意识到回不去了，海滩上有几个朋友。于是我们开始向她们挥手。我们双手举过头顶挥动。

00:12:02.200 --> 00:12:12.760
<v Patty Martin>And our friend just kept waving back. She was standing and talking to some other people on the beach. That must have happened two or three times. And we waved like crazy, and she waved back.
<v Patty Martin>我们的朋友也一直挥手回应。她当时正站在那儿和别人聊天。这种情况肯定发生了两三次。我们疯狂地挥手，她也挥手回应。

00:12:12.760 --> 00:12:28.420
<v Patty Martin>And then when we got on the surfboard later, when the surfer picked us up and we still couldn't get in, we were waving again. And again she thought-- we asked her afterwards, why did you think we were waving? She said, we thought you were just trying to show us you were on the surfboard with this guy.
<v Patty Martin>后来当我们上了冲浪板，冲浪者接我们时我们还是无法上岸，我们又在挥手了。她以为——我们事后问她，为什么以为我们在挥手？她说，我们以为你们只是想展示你们和这家伙在一起冲浪。

00:12:28.420 --> 00:12:51.250
<v Patty Martin>So I'm like, God, we're waving frantically to tell you what, that we're on a surfboard with some 19-year-old, and we're-- Nobody got it. And we were sufficiently panicked. And nobody saw anything but a bunch of middle-aged women at Fat Ladies Beach, waving to their friends.
<v Patty Martin>所以我说，天哪，我们疯狂地挥手是在告诉你什么，告诉你在冲浪板上载着个19岁的小伙子吗？没人明白。我们确实非常恐慌。没人看到别的，只看到一群中年妇女在胖女人海滩向朋友挥手。

00:12:51.250 --> 00:12:56.850
<v Ira Glass>Patty Martin from Nantucket. She talked to Jim Sulzer. She has since passed away.
<v Ira Glass>南塔克特的帕蒂·马丁。她和吉姆·萨尔泽聊过。她后来已经去世了。

00:12:56.850 --> 00:13:07.260
<v Ira Glass>(HOST) IRA GLASS: Act Five-- The Sound of No Hands Clapping. This one came from Vicki Merrick and Eric Kipp and Jay Allison, who's the voice you hear.
<v Ira Glass>第五幕——没有双手鼓掌的声音。这来自于薇琪·梅里克、埃里克·基普和杰·埃里森，你听到的声音就是他的。

00:13:07.260 --> 00:13:08.580
<v Jay Allison>Listen.
<v Jay Allison>听着。

00:13:08.580 --> 00:13:16.330
<v Jay Allison>[WET SUCTION CLAPS]
<v Jay Allison>[湿漉漉的拍打声]

00:13:16.330 --> 00:13:24.060
<v Jay Allison>Scallops clapping on Martha's Vineyard.
<v Jay Allison>玛莎葡萄园岛上扇贝在拍手。

00:13:24.060 --> 00:13:35.750
<v Ira Glass>(HOST) IRA GLASS: Act Six-- Reaching Out With Radio. Blunt Youth Radio is this project where they work with incarcerated teenagers. This story comes from Long Creek Youth Development Center in South Portland, Maine.
<v Ira Glass>第六幕——用广播沟通。布朗特青年电台是一个与被监禁青少年合作的项目。这个故事来自缅因州南波特兰的朗溪青少年发展中心。

00:13:35.750 --> 00:13:51.470
<v Ira Glass>And just imagine how this works-- you're a teenager. You're locked up. You're in juvenile detention. And this group comes to you and says they will help you make your own radio story on a subject that concerns you. What do you do that story about? Well, here's Joey.
<v Ira Glass>想象一下这是怎么运作的——你是个青少年，被关起来了，在少管所。然后这群人来告诉你，他们会帮你制作关于你关心主题的广播故事。你会写什么故事？嗯，听听乔伊的。

00:13:51.470 --> 00:14:11.460
<v Joey>Hi, I'm Joey, and I ate somebody's urine. It all started when I went to dinner at the cafeteria and someone told my friend not to eat the pudding. Then my friend told me, after I ate, that someone had peed in it. I survived that day, but I couldn't stop thinking about it. So I went to the cooks to see if they had an answer.
<v Joey>嗨，我是乔伊，我吃了别人的尿。这一切始于我去餐厅吃晚饭，有人告诉我朋友别吃布丁。然后我吃完后，朋友告诉我，有人往里撒尿了。那一天我活下来了，但我无法停止思考这个问题。所以我去找厨师看看他们有什么解释。

00:14:11.460 --> 00:14:16.400
<v Joey>This is Joey and Jake. We're about to see if we can interview Bill Wyman on--
<v Joey>这是乔伊和杰克。我们正要去采访比尔·怀曼，关于——

00:14:16.400 --> 00:14:18.320
<v Jake>Our food situation.
<v Jake>我们的饮食状况。

00:14:18.320 --> 00:14:20.020
<v Joey>--people messing with our food.
<v Joey>——关于有人在我们的食物里动手动脚。

00:14:20.020 --> 00:14:21.280
<v Jake>Yeah, we're not too happy.
<v Jake>是的，我们很不高兴。

00:14:21.280 --> 00:14:40.120
<v Joey>We're not too happy. Excuse me, Mr. Wyman? I was wondering if I could interview you and talk to you about people putting-- if it happened-- glass in our pancakes and peeing in the pudding and stuff? We was wondering what you had to say about that.
<v Joey>我们很不高兴。打扰一下，怀曼先生？我想采访您，聊聊关于有人往——如果这是真的话——煎饼里放玻璃，往布丁里撒尿之类的事？我们想知道您对此有什么看法。

00:14:40.120 --> 00:14:46.830
<v Bill Wyman>They don't. Nobody does that. You kids don't handle the food at all.
<v Bill Wyman>没有这种事。没人做那种事。你们这些孩子根本接触不到食物。

00:14:46.830 --> 00:14:51.280
<v Joey>We heard that it was brought up at briefing that somebody put glass in the pancakes.
<v Joey>我们听说有人在会上提到有人往煎饼里放了玻璃。

00:14:51.280 --> 00:15:00.610
<v Bill Wyman>No, that wasn't glass. It was a piece of plastic that came from one of the glasses out there in the dining room that you kids had.
<v Bill Wyman>不，那不是玻璃。那是你们这些孩子在餐厅用的一只杯子上的塑料碎片。

00:15:00.610 --> 00:15:02.710
<v Joey>Do you know how it got into the pancake batter?
<v Joey>您知道它怎么进煎饼面糊里的吗？

00:15:02.710 --> 00:15:05.760
<v Bill Wyman>It didn't get into the pancake batter. It was put in there afterwards.
<v Bill Wyman>它没进面糊。是后来放进去的。

00:15:05.760 --> 00:15:08.450
<v Jake>Well, we heard different from other kids.
<v Jake>嗯，我们从别的孩子那里听到的不一样。

00:15:08.450 --> 00:15:10.620
<v Bill Wyman>You hear different? Do you know anything about it?
<v Bill Wyman>你们听到的不一样？你们知道些什么吗？

00:15:10.620 --> 00:15:12.100
<v Joey>We heard that it was--
<v Joey>我们听说它是——

00:15:12.100 --> 00:15:18.400
<v Bill Wyman>Did you two have any idea, whatsoever, what's going on?
<v Bill Wyman>你们两个有任何头绪吗？到底发生了什么？

00:15:18.400 --> 00:15:20.390
<v Jake>That's why we came to you, to get an idea.
<v Jake>所以我们才来找您，想了解一下。

00:15:20.390 --> 00:15:26.530
<v Bill Wyman>No, that's not why you came to me. You came to me because you thought it was true.
<v Bill Wyman>不，那不是你们来找我的原因。你们来找我是因为你们以为那是真的。

00:15:26.530 --> 00:15:28.550
<v Jake>Yeah, of course. If--
<v Jake>是啊，当然。如果——

00:15:28.550 --> 00:15:29.830
<v Bill Wyman>Then you kept it going.
<v Bill Wyman>然后你们还在散播。

00:15:29.830 --> 00:15:35.380
<v Jake>If somebody said that they messed with your food, joking or not, wouldn't you want to know really what happened or not?
<v Jake>如果有人说他们动了你的食物，不管是不是开玩笑，你难道不想知道到底发生了什么吗？

00:15:35.380 --> 00:15:45.030
<v Bill Wyman>No, I would totally go with the kitchen, with the kitchen crew, and what you guys have been told.
<v Bill Wyman>不，我会完全相信厨房团队，相信你们被告知的情况。

00:15:45.030 --> 00:15:53.370
<v Jake>So you'd believe the kitchen crew over the person? No matter how much credibility you can give them, you'd believe the kitchen crew over them? Even if--
<v Jake>所以你会相信厨房团队而不是当事人？不管对方的可信度有多高，你都信厨房团队？即使——

00:15:53.370 --> 00:16:01.420
<v Bill Wyman>Of course, because you don't cook the food. You think it's funny, don't you? You really think it's funny.
<v Bill Wyman>当然，因为又不是你们在做饭。你们觉得这很有趣，对吧？你们真的觉得这很有趣。

00:16:01.420 --> 00:16:03.370
<v Jake>No, I really don't think it's funny.
<v Jake>不，我一点也不觉得有趣。

00:16:03.370 --> 00:16:04.210
<v Bill Wyman>Then why are you grinning like a Cheshire cat?
<v Bill Wyman>那你为什么笑得像只柴郡猫？

00:16:04.210 --> 00:16:09.880
<v Jake>Because you're going friggin' zero to 10 just because we're asking a bunch of questions.
<v Jake>因为我们就问几个问题，您就气急败坏。

00:16:09.880 --> 00:16:18.640
<v Bill Wyman>No, it's what you're asking. It's how you're asking the same thing over again. And you're trying to get an answer, which there isn't any answer there.
<v Bill Wyman>不，是因为你们问的问题。是因为你们一遍又一遍地问同样的事。你们想得到一个根本不存在的答案。

00:16:18.640 --> 00:16:28.430
<v Joey>All right, this is obviously not working. I'm going to have to interview somebody else. I heard some kid got jumped because he supposedly peed in the pudding.
<v Joey>好吧，这显然行不通。我得去采访别人。我听说有个孩子因为据称往布丁里撒尿而被打了。

00:16:28.430 --> 00:16:32.060
<v Bill Wyman>Supposedly. That's the key word right there-- supposedly.
<v Bill Wyman>据称。这就是关键——据称。

00:16:32.060 --> 00:16:46.840
<v Woman>We have an investigator, and he investigated the incident. And we also had the pudding tested. They took it to an independent laboratory and tested it, and it was proven that there was no foreign matter in that pudding at all.
<v Woman>我们有调查员，他调查了这件事。我们还把布丁拿去化验了。他们送到了一家独立实验室进行检测，证明布丁里根本没有异物。

00:16:46.840 --> 00:17:03.550
<v Woman>Now, perhaps you weren't told that because I don't think you're told everything. But it was sad that, because you thought there was, you incited a riot in the dining room and made a big mess that kids had to clean up that had nothing to do with it.
<v Woman>也许没人告诉你们这些，因为我觉得你们什么都不知道。但可悲的是，就因为你们以为有，你们在餐厅引发了一场骚乱，搞得一团糟，那些跟这事完全没关系的孩子还得去清理。

00:17:03.550 --> 00:17:15.200
<v Joey>I don't know what to think. If someone did contaminate the food with bodily fluids, I guess I'd rather not know since I'm stuck here and I have to eat the food. Thank you for your time. This is Joey.
<v Joey>我不知道该怎么想。如果真有人用体液污染了食物，我想我还是不要知道的好，毕竟我被困在这，还得吃这里的饭。谢谢您的时间。这是乔伊。

00:17:15.200 --> 00:17:22.670
<v Ira Glass>Joey-- he was 18 when he recorded that story. His friend Jake was 16.
<v Ira Glass>乔伊——他录这个故事时18岁。他的朋友杰克16岁。

00:17:22.670 --> 00:17:43.770
<v Ira Glass>If you're just tuning in, this is This American Life. We have tossed out our regular way of doing the show this week-- trashed it, chucked it, spurned it. We laugh at it, we spit on its grave. And instead, we are bringing you as many short stories as we can fit into 60 minutes. It is barely, what, 18 minutes into the show, and already we are at Act Seven, an incredible achievement.
<v Ira Glass>如果你刚调到这个频道，这是《美国生活》。本周我们抛弃了常规的节目形式——把它撕碎、扔掉、摒弃。我们嘲笑它，向它的坟墓吐口水。取而代之的是，我们在60分钟内塞进尽可能多的短故事。节目才过去——怎么样，18分钟？我们已经到第七幕了，这简直是个奇迹。

00:17:43.770 --> 00:17:49.030
<v Ira Glass>(HOST) IRA GLASS: Act Seven-- Up Where the Air is Clear. We have this story from Jonathan Goldstein.
<v Ira Glass>第七幕——高处空气清新。我们有这个来自乔纳森·戈德斯坦的故事。

00:17:49.030 --> 00:18:10.180
<v Jonathan Goldstein>Before he ever moved to Gotham City, before he grew into the overweight, obsessive sad sack of his later years, the Penguin was a poet and a dandy who lived in London. He wrote complex villanelles and threw lavish dinner parties at which he only became more charming the more he drank. He wore a monocle, a top hat, and carried an umbrella.
<v Jonathan Goldstein>在搬到哥谭市之前，在他长成后来那种肥胖、痴迷的丧气鬼之前，企鹅人曾是一位居住在伦敦的诗人和花花公子。他写过复杂的维拉内尔诗，举办过奢华的晚宴，酒喝得越多就越迷人。他戴着单片眼镜、高礼帽，随身带着一把雨伞。

00:18:10.180 --> 00:18:33.640
<v Jonathan Goldstein>One evening, at one of his dinner parties, after hours spent sipping absinthe, the Penguin ran up to the roof of his building, opened up his large black umbrella, and leaped off into the air. As he coasted to the ground, he hollered out lines from Blake, stuff about grabbing life by the fat of its stomach and giving it a twist. He was that crazy. He was that bursting with life.
<v Jonathan Goldstein>一天晚上，在一次晚宴上，在喝了几个小时苦艾酒后，企鹅人跑到大楼屋顶，撑开他那把黑色大雨伞，跳进了空气中。当他滑向地面时，他高喊着布莱克的诗句，关于抓住生活胃部脂肪并给它一扭的内容。他就是那么疯狂。他就是那么充满生命力。

00:18:33.640 --> 00:19:01.940
<v Jonathan Goldstein>From that night on, he made it his habit to jump off roofs ever higher, while clutching an umbrella. After a while, he got pretty good at it, too. He saw that by kicking his legs and twisting his back a certain way, he could actually prolong his flight, coasting all over the place, sometimes only landing after several daring minutes aloft.
<v Jonathan Goldstein>从那天起，他养成了抓着雨伞从越来越高的屋顶跳下的习惯。过了一阵子，他竟然跳得相当不错。他发现通过踢腿和以某种方式扭动背部，他实际上可以延长飞行时间，四处滑翔，有时敢在空中冒险飞行几分钟才着陆。

00:19:01.940 --> 00:19:18.770
<v Jonathan Goldstein>It came to pass that the Penguin started hearing more and more about a certain nanny named Mary Poppins. She too, he was told, had been floating around London hanging from an umbrella handle. Everywhere he went, the Penguin kept hearing about her, how it was simply insane that they had not met each other yet.
<v Jonathan Goldstein>后来，企鹅人越来越多地听说了一位叫玛丽·波平斯的保姆。据他说，她也曾撑着一把雨伞在伦敦四处漂浮。无论去哪，企鹅人总能听到关于她的事，说他们还没见过面简直太不可思议了。

00:19:18.770 --> 00:19:56.760
<v Jonathan Goldstein>So finally, a dinner party was arranged by someone who knew them both. And on the evening of the party, the Penguin walked into the drawing room, saw Mary Poppins on the divan, doffed his top hat, and bowed low, as was his style in those days. He'd planned a few things to say and do when first meeting Mary Poppins. He thought he might lift up his umbrella as though challenging her to a duel. He imagined she would smile and take up her own frilly, perhaps pink, umbrella, and then together, they would dance about the room, leaping over furniture, parrying and thrusting, perhaps even winding things up, breathing heavily nose to nose.
<v Jonathan Goldstein>最后，一位认识他们两个人的中间人安排了一场晚宴。在宴会当晚，企鹅人走进客厅，看到沙发上的玛丽·波平斯，脱下高礼帽，深深鞠躬，这在他当年的风格里很常见。他曾计划在初次见到玛丽·波平斯时说些什么、做些什么。他想他或许可以举起伞，好像挑战她进行决斗。他想象她会微笑着拿起她那把花哨、或许是粉红色的雨伞，然后他们一起在房间里跳舞，越过家具，格挡突刺，甚至可能鼻尖对着鼻尖，呼吸急促地结束这一切。

00:19:56.760 --> 00:20:15.410
<v Jonathan Goldstein>Instead what happened was the Penguin became very shy and quiet. As he stood there staring at her, his top hat felt needlessly clumsy, his monocle too small for his face. And the squinting needed to keep it in place was giving him a slight headache. For the first time in his life, the Penguin felt ludicrous.
<v Jonathan Goldstein>结果却是企鹅人变得非常害羞和安静。当他站在那盯着她看时，他的高礼帽感到多余而笨拙，单片眼镜对于他的脸来说太小了。为了固定它而进行的眯眼让他有点头疼。这是企鹅人人生中第一次感到自己很可笑。

00:20:15.410 --> 00:20:39.620
<v Jonathan Goldstein>I imagine you two must have an infinite amount of things to speak of, said their host as he sat them together at the dinner table. The Penguin nodded uncertainly. After three or four minutes, it became clear that the Penguin and Mary Poppins had absolutely nothing to say to one another that did not deal exclusively with umbrella travel-- getting stuck in trees, the shoulder aches, anxiety about tipping over in the wind.
<v Jonathan Goldstein>“我想你们两个一定有聊不完的话题，”他们的主人让他们坐在一起吃饭时说道。企鹅人不安地点了点头。三四分钟后，很明显，企鹅人和玛丽·波平斯根本没有什么可说的，除非全是关于雨伞旅行——被卡在树里、肩膀酸痛、担心在风中翻倒。

00:20:39.620 --> 00:21:00.640
<v Jonathan Goldstein>Everyone at the table just sat there, staring at them expectantly, which made the whole thing even more awkward. Trying to move things along, Mary Poppins asked the Penguin if he liked to sing, to which the Penguin responded, only when I'm drunk. Then she asked if he enjoyed children, to which he replied, yes, in a sweet wine sauce.
<v Jonathan Goldstein>桌上的每个人都坐在那，期待地盯着他们，这让场面更尴尬了。为了打破僵局，玛丽·波平斯问企鹅人喜不喜欢唱歌，企鹅人回答说：只有喝醉的时候。然后她问他喜不喜欢孩子，他回答说：喜欢，用甜酒酱烹饪的那种。

00:21:00.640 --> 00:21:36.480
<v Jonathan Goldstein>The Penguin then asked Mary Poppins how she kept people from looking up her skirt when she flew. She smiled politely, then turned to the man on her left and asked him how he was enjoying the lamb. The man on her left was wearing an elegant, aristocratic cape. Mary, a bit drunk on the sherry, noted that if he spread his cape out, he might be able to glide about like a bat. The man on her left chuckled and suggested that, after dinner, they head up to the roof and give it a try, which they did.
<v Jonathan Goldstein>企鹅人接着问玛丽·波平斯当她飞行时是怎么防止别人看她裙子底下的。她礼貌地笑了笑，然后转向左边的男人，问他觉得羊肉怎么样。她左边的男人穿着优雅的贵族披风。玛丽喝了点雪利酒，有点上头，随口说如果他把披风撑开，或许能像蝙蝠一样滑翔。她左边的男人笑了笑，建议餐后他们去屋顶试试，于是他们真的去了。

00:21:36.480 --> 00:21:50.100
<v Ira Glass>Jonathan Goldstein-- he's the host of the podcast Heavyweight, which feels exactly like this story except the people in it are not fictional.
<v Ira Glass>乔纳森·戈德斯坦——他是播客《重量级》的主持人，感觉就像这个故事一样，只是里面的人不是虚构的。

00:21:50.100 --> 00:22:02.210
<v Ira Glass>(HOST) IRA GLASS: Act Eight-- the Greatest Dog Name in The World. Yes, we have the true story of its origin. Years ago, an exclusive told by two brothers, one of whom is 12, the other is 13.
<v Ira Glass>第八幕——世界上最伟大的狗名。没错，我们要讲这个名字起源的真实故事。几年前，由两兄弟讲述的独家报道，一个12岁，另一个13岁。

00:22:02.210 --> 00:22:13.450
<v Valion Lotze>I wanted to name him Pasta. I used to like pasta a lot. And it was probably the first thing that came to my mind, so out of nowhere, I said Pasta. So he said Batman.
<v Valion Lotze>我想叫他“意大利面”。我以前很喜欢意大利面。这可能是我想到的第一件事，所以毫无预兆地我就说了“意大利面”。于是他说“蝙蝠侠”。

00:22:13.450 --> 00:22:22.280
<v Paris Lotze>I wanted to name him Batman because I saw, in a movie, the dog stuck his head out the window and his ears went straight up. And it looked like-- it reminded me of Batman.
<v Paris Lotze>我想叫他“蝙蝠侠”是因为我看电影时，狗狗把头伸出窗外，耳朵竖得直直的。看起来——让我想到了蝙蝠侠。

00:22:22.280 --> 00:22:25.210
<v Valion Lotze>And we fought over it for a little bit.
<v Valion Lotze>我们为此争论了一会儿。

00:22:25.210 --> 00:22:46.140
<v Paris Lotze>I just remember running around and chasing each other. I was jumping on my mom's bed, saying, Batman, Batman, Batman. And then my brother was sitting in the chair, saying, Pasta, Pasta, Pasta. And that must have gone on for an hour.
<v Paris Lotze>我只记得我们在四处乱跑、互相追逐。我跳上妈妈的床，喊着：蝙蝠侠，蝙蝠侠，蝙蝠侠。然后我弟弟坐在椅子上，喊着：意大利面，意大利面，意大利面。这肯定持续了一个小时。

00:22:46.140 --> 00:22:59.640
<v Valion Lotze>I think it was right around the time we had this big fight about gumballs, which I'm not going to get into because it was pretty embarrassing. But if there was just some little thing that we couldn't agree on, then it would just blew up into this whole big thing.
<v Valion Lotze>我想那正好是我们为口香糖大吵一架的时候，我不打算细说，因为挺尴尬的。但如果只是为了某件我们无法达成一致的小事，它就会演变成一件大闹剧。

00:22:59.640 --> 00:23:11.170
<v Paris Lotze>Yeah, I remember being pretty upset about it. And then my mom comes in and says, all right, that's it. It's over. It's Pasta Batman. That's it.
<v Paris Lotze>是的，我记得为此很沮丧。然后我妈走进来，说：行了，就这样。名字就是“意大利面蝙蝠侠”。就这样。

00:23:11.170 --> 00:23:22.530
<v Valion Lotze>And then there was silence. And then from there, he was Pasta Batman Lotze. Pasta. [CLAPS] Come on, Pasta Batman.
<v Valion Lotze>然后是一阵沉默。从那时起，他就成了“意大利面蝙蝠侠·洛茨”。意大利面。[拍手] 来吧，意大利面蝙蝠侠。

00:23:22.530 --> 00:23:35.590
<v Paris Lotze>Pasta, here. Pasta Batman. Hey, good doggie.
<v Paris Lotze>意大利面，过来。意大利面蝙蝠侠。嘿，好狗狗。

00:23:35.590 --> 00:23:43.360
<v Ira Glass>Their names? Valion and Paris Lotze, age 12 and 13. They spoke with Katia Dunn.
