<v Ira Glass>Greg Allen and Heather Riordan from Too Much Light Makes the Baby Go Blind: 30 Plays in 60 Minutes, which ran every weekend in Chicago for 28 years until it ended its run in 2016. The Neo-Futurists have a new show, which also features lots of little two-minute plays. It is called The Infinite Wrench. Their website-- neo-futurists.org.
<v Ira Glass>格雷格·艾伦和希瑟·里奥丹的《太多的光会让婴儿失明：60分钟30出戏》，该剧在芝加哥每个周末上演28年，直到2016年结束。“新未来主义者”有一个新节目，也包含很多两分钟的小戏，叫《无限扳手》。他们的网站是 neo-futurists.org。

00:27:59.730 --> 00:28:16.420
<v Ira Glass>Coming up, David Sedaris on an important-- and I have to say-- undiscussed question about cell phone use, and so many, so many other little stories, I don't even want to count them, in a minute from Chicago Public Radio when our program continues.
<v Ira Glass>接下来，大卫·塞达里斯将探讨关于手机使用的一个重要且没人讨论过的问题，以及更多、更多的小故事，我都不想数了，一分钟后，当我们的节目继续时，芝加哥公共广播。

00:28:16.420 --> 00:28:29.410
<v Ira Glass>(HOST) IRA GLASS: Well, this is turning out to be an interesting show, isn't it? It's This American Life. I'm Ira Glass. And if you're just tuning in, this week we are throwing out our regular way of doing things-- you know, three or four stories on some theme, blah, blah, blah. To hell with all that.
<v Ira Glass>第十一幕——好吧，这节目变得越来越有趣了，不是吗？这里是《美国生活》。我是艾拉·格拉斯。如果你刚调到这个频道，本周我们抛弃了常规的做事方式——你知道，围绕一个主题讲三四个故事什么的，见鬼去吧。

00:28:29.410 --> 00:28:42.950
<v Ira Glass>Instead, we are trying to cram as many stories into one hour as we humanly can. We guarantee 20. There may be more. We are at number 11, Etiquette Lesson. Here's writer David Sedaris.
<v Ira Glass>相反，我们试图在这一小时内塞进尽可能多的故事。我们保证会有20个。可能更多。我们现在进行到第11号，“礼仪课”。这里是作家大卫·塞达里斯。

00:28:42.950 --> 00:29:03.080
<v David Sedaris>In a men's room at LaGuardia Airport, I watched a man take his cell phone from his jacket pocket, step into an empty stall, and proceed to dial. I thought he had come for the relative privacy, but looking through the space beneath the door, I saw that his pants were gathered about his ankles. He was sitting on the toilet.
<v David Sedaris>在拉瓜迪亚机场的男厕所里，我看到一个男人从夹克口袋里掏出手机，走进一个空隔间，开始拨号。我还以为他只是为了找个相对私密的空间，但透过门下的缝隙看进去，我看到他的裤子堆在脚踝处。他正坐在马桶上。

00:29:03.080 --> 00:29:16.780
<v David Sedaris>Most airport calls began with geography. I'm in Kansas City, people say. I'm in Houston. I'm at Kennedy. When asked where he was, the man on the phone said simply, I'm at the airport. What do you think?
<v David Sedaris>大多数机场通话都是从地理位置开始的。“我在堪萨斯城，”人们会说。“我在休斯顿。”“我在肯尼迪机场。”当被问到在哪时，电话里的那个人简单地说：“我在机场。你觉得呢？”

00:29:16.780 --> 00:29:36.600
<v David Sedaris>The sounds of a public toilet are not the sounds you would generally associate with an airport, and so his 'what do you think' struck me as unfair. The person he was talking to obviously felt the same way. What do you mean what airport, the man said. I'm at LaGuardia. Now put me through to Marty.
<v David Sedaris>公共厕所的声音通常不会让人联想到机场，所以他的“你觉得呢”让我觉得很不公平。电话那头的人显然也这么想。“什么叫什么机场，”那人说。“我在拉瓜迪亚。现在帮我转接马蒂。”

00:29:36.600 --> 00:30:00.460
<v David Sedaris>A few hours later, I was in Boston relating the story to my sister, Tiffany. I mean, he actually placed a call while sitting on the toilet. Tiffany is big on rules, and so I expected a certain degree of outrage. I wanted disgust, but instead, she said only, I don't believe in cell phones. But you do believe in talking on the phone while sitting on the toilet?
<v David Sedaris>几个小时后，我在波士顿向我姐姐蒂芙尼讲述了这个故事。我说，他是坐在马桶上打电话的。蒂芙尼非常讲究规矩，所以我预料她会有一定程度的愤慨。我想要的是厌恶，但她只说：“我不相信手机。” “那你相信坐在马桶上打电话吗？”

00:30:00.460 --> 00:30:18.590
<v David Sedaris>Well, it's not a belief, she said, but I mean, sure. When asked how she explains the noise, Tiffany scrunched up her face and held an imaginary receiver to her mouth. I say, (STRAINING) don't mind me, I'm just trying to get the lid off this jar.
<v David Sedaris>“嗯，这不是信不信的问题，”她说，“但我的意思是，当然。当被问到怎么解释那些噪音时，”蒂芙尼皱起脸，对着嘴做了一个假想的听筒。“我会说，（用力地）别介意，我只是想把这个罐子的盖子拧开。”

00:30:18.590 --> 00:30:50.310
<v David Sedaris>Her face returned to normal and I thought of all the times I had fallen for that line, all the times I had pictured my sister standing helpless in her kitchen. Try tapping the lid against the countertop, I'd said, or rinse it in hot water, that sometimes works. Eventually, after much struggle, she would let out a breath. There we go, she'd say. I've got it now. And then she would say thank you. And I'd hang up thinking, well, it's a good thing she called me.
<v David Sedaris>她的脸恢复正常，我想起我曾经多少次被这句话骗倒，多少次想象我姐姐在厨房里手足无措。“试着在台面上敲敲盖子，”我说过，“或者用热水冲一下，有时管用。”最终，经过一番挣扎，她会呼出一口气。“好了，”她会说。“现在弄开了。”然后她会说谢谢。而我挂断电话时会想，好吧，幸好她给我打了电话。

00:30:50.310 --> 00:30:55.020
<v Ira Glass>David Sedaris. His new book Calypso comes out this May.
<v Ira Glass>大卫·塞达里斯。他的新书《卡吕普索》将于今年五月出版。

00:30:55.020 --> 00:31:02.100
<v Ira Glass>(HOST) IRA GLASS: Act 12-- To Tell the Truth. This was recorded by Brent Runyon in the kids' section of the public library.
<v Ira Glass>第十二幕——说真话。这是布伦特·鲁尼恩在公共图书馆童书区录制的。

00:31:02.100 --> 00:31:06.030
<v Kid 1>My parents punish me so many times because I lie.
<v Kid 1>我父母惩罚我很多次，因为我说谎。

00:31:06.030 --> 00:31:07.130
<v Kid 1>[LAUGHTER]
<v Kid 1>[笑声]

00:31:07.130 --> 00:31:16.710
<v Kid 1>Well, I was home one day and I tripped over my brother-- well, OK, don't tell anybody this. I jumped over my brother's chair. I jumped--
<v Kid 1>好吧，有一天我在家，我在我兄弟身上绊倒了——好吧，行了，别告诉任何人。我跳过了我兄弟的椅子。我跳——

00:31:16.710 --> 00:31:17.550
<v Kid 2>You did?
<v Kid 2>真的吗？

00:31:17.550 --> 00:31:17.970
<v Kid 1>Yeah.
<v Kid 1>真的。

00:31:17.970 --> 00:31:18.990
<v Kid 2>You lied to me?
<v Kid 2>你对我撒谎了？

00:31:18.990 --> 00:31:20.370
<v Kid 1>I told you I jumped.
<v Kid 1>我告诉你我跳过去了。

00:31:20.370 --> 00:31:21.180
<v Kid 2>No, you didn't.
<v Kid 2>不，你没有。

00:31:21.180 --> 00:31:22.260
<v Kid 1>I told you the first day I got my crutches.
<v Kid 1>我拿到拐杖的第一天就告诉你了。

00:31:22.260 --> 00:31:23.830
<v Kid 2>No, you did not.
<v Kid 2>不，你绝对没有。

00:31:23.830 --> 00:31:25.240
<v Kid 1>Oh.
<v Kid 1>哦。

00:31:25.240 --> 00:31:34.050
<v Ira Glass>(HOST) IRA GLASS: Act 13-- More Lies. This happened to Catherine and her husband John long before they were married, back before they graduated from college.
<v Ira Glass>第十三幕——更多的谎言。这件事发生在凯瑟琳和她丈夫约翰结婚很久之前，还在他们大学毕业之前。

00:31:34.050 --> 00:31:50.550
<v Catherine>Girls babysit a lot and boys don't. And girls understand that, when you babysit, part of the deal is that you get to eat anything you want. So after we'd put the kids to bed, I said, well, we should go see what they have to eat.
<v Catherine>女孩子经常做保姆，男孩子不做。女孩子明白，当你做保姆时，交易的一部分就是你可以随便吃你想吃的任何东西。所以在把孩子们送上床后，我说，嗯，我们该去看看他们有什么吃的。

00:31:50.550 --> 00:31:59.170
<v Catherine>And he said, we can't eat their food. And I said, of course we can eat their food. What do you mean? And he said, that's stealing.
<v Catherine>他说，我们不能吃他们的食物。我说，我们当然可以吃。你什么意思？他说，那是偷窃。

00:31:59.170 --> 00:32:08.950
<v Catherine>I said, John, I promise you, it's fine to eat something. They expect us to. They understand that-- they don't expect us to starve while we're babysitting.
<v Catherine>我说，约翰，我向你保证，吃点东西没关系。他们指望我们吃。他们明白——他们不希望我们在做保姆时挨饿。

00:32:08.950 --> 00:32:20.710
<v Catherine>And finally, he said, well, we can eat something, but only something they won't miss. And they had a huge crate full of grapefruits, and they also had cans and cans of black beans.
<v Catherine>最后，他说，好吧，我们可以吃点东西，但只能吃他们不会发现少掉的东西。他们有一大箱葡萄柚，还有几罐黑豆。

00:32:20.710 --> 00:32:22.640
<v Ira Glass>[LAUGHS]
<v Ira Glass>[笑]

00:32:22.640 --> 00:32:49.020
<v Catherine>[LAUGHS] So I had half a grapefruit. And John opened up a can of black beans and had that. And then I wrapped up the other half of the grapefruit. And John cleaned-- well, rinsed out and dried off the empty can of black beans. And we put the wrapped-up half a grapefruit and the cleaned out can of black beans in his bag.
<v Catherine>[笑] 所以我吃了半个葡萄柚。约翰打开了一罐黑豆吃掉了。然后我把剩下的一半葡萄柚包起来。约翰把空黑豆罐洗干净擦干。我们把包好的半个葡萄柚和洗干净的空黑豆罐放进他的包里。

00:32:49.020 --> 00:32:51.800
<v Ira Glass>[LAUGHING] So that people wouldn't know that you had eaten anything?
<v Ira Glass>[大笑] 所以别人就不会知道你们吃了什么？

00:32:51.800 --> 00:32:54.090
<v Catherine>We had destroyed the evidence. And this--
<v Catherine>我们销毁了证据。而这——

00:32:54.090 --> 00:32:54.820
<v Ira Glass>At his insistence?
<v Ira Glass>是在他的坚持下？

00:32:54.820 --> 00:33:07.960
<v Catherine>Yes, it was absurd. And then we watched TV, our hunger satisfied. And then the couple came home, and we made small talk.
<v Catherine>是的，这太荒谬了。然后我们看了电视，填饱了肚子。接着那对夫妇回家了，我们闲聊了一会儿。

00:33:07.960 --> 00:33:33.190
<v Catherine>And then John picked up his bag in the hallway and there was a sort of dull thud, and half the grapefruit fell out on the floor. And I said, oh, that's mine. I'm sorry, I-- uh, we're allowed to take a piece of fruit from the dining hall. And I had taken that grapefruit from the dining hall, and that's why I have it here.
<v Catherine>然后约翰在走廊拿起包，发出了一声沉闷的撞击声，半个葡萄柚掉在地板上。我说，哦，那是我的。对不起，我……呃，我们被允许从食堂带一个水果。我从食堂拿了那个葡萄柚，这就是为什么我有那个。

00:33:33.190 --> 00:33:52.240
<v Catherine>And then they sort of said, oh, OK, OK, that's nice. And then I put it back in John's bag. And then John picked up his bag again, and there was-- you guessed it-- a clang. And clanging out onto the floor went this empty can of black beans.
<v Catherine>然后他们就说，哦，好的，好的，那挺好。然后我把它放回约翰的包里。接着约翰又拿起包，就——你猜对了——发出了一声当啷声。那个空黑豆罐当啷着掉在地板上。

00:33:52.240 --> 00:34:08.370
<v Catherine>And when the can fell out on the floor, John said, oh, that's mine. I keep change in that. Like, I keep change-- [LAUGHS] as if that was less insane.
<v Catherine>罐子掉地上时，约翰说，哦，那是我的。我把零钱放在里面。就像，我把零钱放在里面——[笑] 好像这听起来没那么疯狂。

00:34:08.370 --> 00:34:11.850
<v Ira Glass>[LAUGHTER]
<v Ira Glass>[笑声]

00:34:11.850 --> 00:34:25.740
<v Ira Glass>(HOST) IRA GLASS: Act 14-- Call in Colonel Mustard For Questioning, Or That's What Happens If You Don't Use a Condiment, Kids. See all the stupid jokes that you end up telling if you have a story that takes place in a hot dog factory about hot dogs? Here we go.
<v Ira Glass>第十四幕——叫芥末上校来审问，或者这就是不加调料的结果，孩子们。看看如果你在热狗工厂里讲热狗的故事，会讲出多少愚蠢的笑话。开始吧。

00:34:25.740 --> 00:34:43.330
<v Jim Bodman>My name is Jim Bodman and I'm the chairman of the Vienna Sausage Company in Chicago. And the building that we are currently standing in, which is on the north side of Chicago, on Damen near the corner of Fullerton, was built around 1970.
<v Jim Bodman>我叫吉姆·博德曼，我是芝加哥维也纳香肠公司的主席。我们目前站的这座大楼，位于芝加哥北区，达门大街靠近富勒顿街角，建于1970年左右。

00:34:43.330 --> 00:34:46.570
<v Ira Glass>This hot dog plant, Jim Bodman says, replaced the company's original facility.
<v Ira Glass>吉姆·博德曼说，这个热狗工厂取代了公司最初的设施。

00:34:46.570 --> 00:35:10.480
<v Jim Bodman>So it was put together in a Rube Goldberg kind of arrangement. So we moved into this building. And this was a brand new, state of the art, stainless steel, refrigeration is perfect, spit clean building. And we started making our natural, old-world hickory smoked, natural-casing hot dogs here. And it wasn't as good.
<v Jim Bodman>所以它是以鲁布·戈德堡式的安排拼凑在一起的。我们搬进了这栋大楼。这是全新的、最先进的、不锈钢的、制冷完美、一尘不染的大楼。我们开始在这里制作我们的天然、旧世界山核桃木烟熏、天然肠衣热狗。但味道没那么好了。

00:35:10.480 --> 00:35:23.100
<v Ira Glass>They tasted OK, he says, but they didn't have the right snap when you bit into them. And even worse, the color was wrong. The hot dogs were all pink instead of bright red. So they tried to figure out what was wrong.
<v Ira Glass>他说，味道还可以，但咬下去没有那种正确的弹性。更糟的是，颜色不对。热狗全是粉红色的，而不是鲜红色。所以他们试图找出问题所在。

00:35:23.100 --> 00:35:34.480
<v Ira Glass>The ingredients were all the same, the spices were all the same, the process was all the same. Maybe it was the temperature in the smokehouse. Maybe the water on the north side of Chicago wasn't the same as the water on the South side.
<v Ira Glass>配料都一样，调料都一样，流程也一样。也许是烟熏房的温度问题。也许是芝加哥北区的水和南区的不一样。

00:35:34.480 --> 00:35:39.730
<v Ira Glass>They searched. They searched for a year and a half. Nothing checked out.
<v Ira Glass>他们搜寻。他们找了一年半。什么都没发现。

00:35:39.730 --> 00:36:00.820
<v Ira Glass>Then one night, a bunch of guys from the plant are out having a drink, gabbing about the good old days, back in the old plant on Maxwell Street. They start talking about this guy named Irving, one of those guys who knows everybody in the plant, has nicknames for everybody. And listen to what Irving's job was. Every day, he would weave his way with the uncooked sausages through the maze of passageways in the old plant.
<v Ira Glass>然后一天晚上，工厂里的一帮伙计出去喝酒，聊起在麦克斯韦街老工厂的往事。他们开始谈论一个叫欧文的家伙，那种在工厂里认识每个人、给每个人起绰号的人。听听欧文的工作是什么。每天，他都要穿过老工厂错综复杂的通道，带着未煮熟的香肠穿梭。

00:36:00.820 --> 00:36:21.250
<v Jim Bodman>He would go through the hanging vents. That's where we hang the pastrami pieces, and it's quite warm. And he would go through the boiler room, where we produced all the energy for the plant. He would go next to the tanks where we cook the corned beef, finally get around the corner, and in some cases, actually go up an elevator. And then he would be at the smokehouse. He would put it in the smokehouse and he would cook it.
<v Jim Bodman>他会经过悬挂通风口。那是我们挂熏牛肉的地方，非常暖和。他会经过锅炉房，我们为工厂生产能量的地方。他会经过我们煮咸牛肉的罐子旁边，最后绕过拐角，在某些情况下，甚至要坐电梯。然后他会到达烟熏房。他会把它放进烟熏房，然后把它煮熟。

00:36:21.250 --> 00:36:32.350
<v Ira Glass>And as they were telling stories about Irving-- Irving this, Irving that-- a light bulb goes off. In the fancy new modern plant, there was no Irving. Irving didn't want to commute to the north side.
<v Ira Glass>就在他们讲欧文的故事时——欧文这，欧文那——灯泡亮了。在豪华的新现代化工厂里，没有欧文。欧文不想通勤去北区。

00:36:32.350 --> 00:36:52.820
<v Ira Glass>There was no maze of hallways. There was no half-hour trip where the sausage would get warm before they would cook it. In the new plant, they just stuffed the sausages in a cold room and cooked them in a smokehouse in the room next door to it. Irving's trip was the secret ingredient that made the dogs red. So secret, even the guys who ran the plant didn't know about it.
<v Ira Glass>没有迷宫般的走廊。没有那半小时的旅程，香肠在煮之前不会变热。在新工厂，他们只是把香肠塞进冷库，然后在隔壁房间的烟熏房煮熟。欧文的旅程是让热狗变红的秘密配料。如此隐秘，连管理工厂的人都不知道。

00:36:52.820 --> 00:37:00.130
<v Jim Bodman>So we said, oh, my God, that is, of course, the reason. Why didn't we know that? That's the dumbest thing in the world to not realize. It's right there.
<v Jim Bodman>所以我们说，天哪，当然是这个原因。我们怎么会不知道？这是世界上最笨的事情，居然没意识到。它就在那。

00:37:00.130 --> 00:37:11.590
<v Jim Bodman>How do we fix it? And the solution to the problem was the room that we're standing in right now. And this was a new addition put onto the plant about two years after we built the facility.
<v Jim Bodman>我们怎么修复它？这个问题的解决方案就是我们现在站的这个房间。这是我们在大楼建成约两年后增加的设施。

00:37:11.590 --> 00:37:12.910
<v Ira Glass>So you had to build this room?
<v Ira Glass>所以你必须建这个房间？

00:37:12.910 --> 00:37:22.990
<v Jim Bodman>This whole room-- the outside bearing wall is that wall right there. We put this whole room on. And in this room, we emulate the old area of the old plant.
<v Jim Bodman>整个房间——外面的承重墙就是那面墙。我们加建了整个房间。在这个房间里，我们模拟了老工厂的旧区域。

00:37:22.990 --> 00:37:27.250
<v Ira Glass>And so this room, essentially, is to simulate Irving?
<v Ira Glass>所以这个房间本质上是为了模拟欧文？

00:37:27.250 --> 00:37:32.380
<v Jim Bodman>That's exactly right. We should have called it Irving-- Irving's Corner.
<v Jim Bodman>完全正确。我们本应该叫它“欧文”——“欧文角”。

00:37:32.380 --> 00:38:15.370
<v Ira Glass>It's warm in Irving's corner, and it smells nice, too-- smoky, like hickory smoke and spices. Since I first heard this story years ago on a tour of this very plant, I found myself telling it now and then. I think that what I love about it is the fact that these guys at the factory had done everything right-- finally built their dream factory with the best equipment and expertise that money could buy. But you can't think of everything. Sometimes you have no idea why you were a success in the first place.
<v Ira Glass>“欧文角”很暖和，闻起来也很棒——烟熏，像山核桃木烟味和香料。自从几年前我参观这家工厂时第一次听到这个故事，我偶尔就会讲它。我想我喜欢它是因为这些工厂的人做对了所有事——最终用金钱能买到的最好的设备和专业知识建造了他们的梦想工厂。但你不可能想到所有事情。有时你根本不知道当初为什么会成功。

00:38:15.370 --> 00:38:34.280
<v Ira Glass>(HOST) IRA GLASS: Act 15-- Mr. Prediction. In the mid-1980s, right out of college, David Rakoff moved to Japan and pretty soon ended up in this office job where he was convinced that he understood a secret about the company and its business that nobody else, not even the big bosses at the company, could see. It was like that from the start.
<v Ira Glass>第十五幕——预测先生。80年代中期，大学刚毕业，大卫·拉科夫搬到了日本，很快得到了一份办公室工作，他深信自己掌握了一个关于公司及其业务的秘密，别人甚至连公司的大老板们都看不出来。一开始就是这样。

00:38:34.280 --> 00:39:02.500
<v David Rakoff>Primarily, the office was an advertising agency. But what they were setting up was this thing for expatriates who were living in Tokyo at the time, or perhaps all of Japan. And it was like a network on a computer. And they would set up a newsletter on the network, and people could quote, "log on" to the computer and talk to one another, or do research.
<v David Rakoff>主要是，这是一家广告代理公司。但他们当时正在为生活在东京，或者可能整个日本的外籍人士建立这个东西。它就像一台电脑上的网络。他们会在网络上建立一个通讯，人们可以引用，“登录”到电脑上互相交谈，或者做研究。

00:39:02.500 --> 00:39:37.200
<v David Rakoff>And I was just-- I don't know, I just looked around the room and I saw these computers and could only think, what kind of loser would log on to a computer, talk to someone? Then, in fact, that night in my diary, I had written something like, this is like those comic book enthusiasts who actually read the little instructions at the bottom of the panel that said, for more on the Green Goblin, check out Spidey #137, from the editor.
<v David Rakoff>我只是——我不知道，我环顾房间，看到这些电脑，只能想，什么样的人会登录电脑去跟别人说话？后来，那一晚在日记里，我写道：“这就像那些真正的漫画书爱好者阅读面板底部的小指令，说：更多关于绿魔的信息，请查看《蜘蛛侠》第137期，编辑留。”

00:39:37.200 --> 00:39:37.620
<v Ira Glass>[LAUGHS]
<v Ira Glass>[笑]

00:39:37.620 --> 00:40:11.490
<v David Rakoff>And in almost the only moment of decisiveness in my entire adult life-- I've certainly never equaled this-- I went in the next morning and I quit. And all I could think was, sayonara, suckers. Good luck with your network. And we know exactly what the network was. It was the internet.
<v David Rakoff>在我成年生活中几乎唯一一次果断时刻中——我当然从未达到过这种水平——我第二天早上进去就辞职了。我当时想的只是：永别了，傻瓜们。祝你们的网络好运。而我们确切地知道那网络是什么。它是互联网。

00:40:11.490 --> 00:40:29.640
<v David Rakoff>I have a negative capacity to identify trends. Like, when in college, I went to see Madonna at Danceteria, which was a club downtown, in like 1982 or whatever. And I thought, boy, is she lousy.
<v David Rakoff>识别趋势，我有着负面的能力。就像大学时，我去Danceteria看麦当娜表演，那是个市中心的俱乐部，大概是1982年吧。我心想，天哪，她真烂。

00:40:29.640 --> 00:40:32.800
<v Ira Glass>Are there other examples besides Madonna and the internet?
<v Ira Glass>除了麦当娜和互联网还有别的例子吗？

00:40:32.800 --> 00:40:50.950
<v David Rakoff>Other than Madonna and the internet, you need another example? When I was an editorial assistant working in publishing, I was handed a manuscript to read. I think I wrote something like, sub-literate, borderline misogyny, an easy pass.
<v David Rakoff>除了麦当娜和互联网，你还需要别的例子？当我做出版业编辑助理时，我收到一份手稿要读。我想我当时写了类似的话：“文化程度低下，边缘厌女症，轻松拒绝。”

00:40:50.950 --> 00:40:58.620
<v David Rakoff>And somebody thought, I'm just going to take a look at this anyway. It was Men Are From Mars, Women Are From Venus.
<v David Rakoff>有人心想，我还是打算看一眼。那是《男人来自火星，女人来自金星》。

00:40:58.620 --> 00:41:00.980
<v Ira Glass>[LAUGHS]
<v Ira Glass>[笑]

00:41:00.980 --> 00:41:06.620
<v David Rakoff>These are not like me saying, I don't think Alicia Silverstone is going to be very good in Clueless. I mean, these are like--
<v David Rakoff>这些不像我说“我不认为艾丽西亚·希尔维斯通在《独领风骚》里会表现很好”。我的意思是，这些是像——

00:41:06.620 --> 00:41:08.740
<v Ira Glass>Pretty big, iconic ones.
<v Ira Glass>相当大、标志性的例子。

00:41:08.740 --> 00:41:24.940
<v David Rakoff>Yeah. It was like, have you fellows heard that crazy lunatic in the market place inveighing against the Pharisees? He'll burn off like so much morning fog. We'll never hear about him ever again. It's just like that.
<v David Rakoff>是的。就像，“伙计们，你们听说那个在市场上斥责法利赛人的疯子吗？他会像晨雾一样散去的。我们永远不会再听说关于他的事了。”就是那样。

00:41:24.940 --> 00:41:40.720
<v Ira Glass>[LAUGHS]
<v Ira Glass>[笑]

00:41:40.720 --> 00:41:51.500
<v Ira Glass>David Rakoff-- the final book that he wrote before he died in 2012 is a book I just love. It's a novel in rhymed couplets-- Love, Dishonor, Marry, Die, Cherish, Perish: A Novel by David Rakoff.
<v Ira Glass>大卫·拉科夫——他在2012年去世前写出的最后一本书是我所钟爱的。这是一部押韵对联的小说——《爱、羞辱、婚姻、死亡、珍惜、毁灭：大卫·拉科夫小说》。

00:41:51.500 --> 00:42:16.080
<v Ira Glass>(HOST) IRA GLASS: You're listening to This American Life, where today, it is all about speed. We are, what, 43 minutes into the program and we have already finished 15 acts? And this brings us to Act 16-- That One Guy at the Office. So if you work at a big office, you know that there's always at least one person whose name you do not know. In Jordana's office, Matt is that guy for, perhaps, as best as anybody can figure, half the people who work there. Jordana will tell you about it.
<v Ira Glass>你正在收听《美国生活》，今天，一切都是为了速度。我们已经过去43分钟了，完成了15幕？这带我们到了第16幕——办公室里的那个人。如果你在大公司工作，你知道总有一个人你不知道他的名字。在乔达娜的办公室里，马特就是那个人，对于办公室里一半的人来说，据大家所能推断的。乔达娜会告诉你。

00:42:16.080 --> 00:42:18.010
<v Ira Glass>[PRINTER PRINTING]
<v Ira Glass>[打印机打印声]

00:42:18.010 --> 00:42:37.500
<v Jordana Gustafson>Matt Ostrower sits next to the printer in the busiest hallway at our office. People walk by him dozens of times a week on their way to retrieve printouts. And though he actually works in the New Media department and has nothing to do with the printer, most people don't know this. It's his sad fate that most of his conversations at work are about one thing.
<v Jordana Gustafson>马特·奥斯特罗威尔坐在办公室最繁忙走廊的打印机旁边。人们每周经过他几十次去拿打印出来的资料。虽然他实际上在“新媒体”部门工作，跟打印机毫无关系，但大多数人不知道这点。他悲剧的命运在于，他在办公室的大部分谈话都关于一件事。

00:42:37.500 --> 00:42:53.210
<v Matt Ostrower>Originally a lot of them were printer-based-- why is this printer taking so long? Oh, the paper's out. Oh, this printer jammed. Some of it's never really left that genre of conversation. They don't really spend too much-- so a lot of it's just very superficial.
<v Matt Ostrower>起初很多都是基于打印机的——为什么这台打印机这么慢？哦，没纸了。哦，打印机卡纸了。有些谈话从未脱离那个类别。他们真的没花太多——所以很多都是非常肤浅的。

00:42:53.210 --> 00:42:57.750
<v Employee>Hey, did you just throw away any printouts here?
<v Employee>嘿，你刚才在这扔过什么打印出来的资料吗？

00:42:57.750 --> 00:42:59.480
<v Matt Ostrower>No, no, I didn't touch anything.
<v Matt Ostrower>不，不，我什么都没碰。

00:42:59.480 --> 00:43:12.070
<v Jordana Gustafson>I'd been working in the office a few months when, one day, a friend called me and said he was hanging out with one of my coworkers who lives in his building. Who, I asked? Matt, he said. I had no idea who that was and said so.
<v Jordana Gustafson>我在办公室工作了几个月后，有一天，一个朋友打电话给我说他正跟一个住在他那栋楼的同事在一起。我问：谁？马特，他说。我根本不知道那是谁，就说了出来。

00:43:12.070 --> 00:43:25.830
<v Jordana Gustafson>Then I heard a voice in the background say, tell her I sit next to the printer. And that's when his predicament hit me. So I decided to survey my coworkers to see if they knew who he is, what his real job is. Do they even know his name?
<v Jordana Gustafson>然后我听到背景里有个声音说：告诉她我坐在打印机旁边。就在那时，他的困境击中了我。所以我决定调查一下我的同事，看他们是否知道他是谁，他的真实工作是什么。他们甚至知道他的名字吗？

00:43:25.830 --> 00:43:31.590
<v Employee>No, I mean, I know his face very well. I stop, I chat. I say, 'hi, how are you' as I'm grabbing things off the printer.
<v Employee>不，我的意思是，我非常熟悉他的脸。我停下来，聊天。当我从打印机拿东西时，我会说：“嗨，你好吗？”

00:43:31.590 --> 00:43:44.100
<v Employee>I ask him about his little electronic music devices and all that, and we chitchat. And I'd say I do that probably about three or four times a day at least. But I have no idea what his name is.
<v Employee>我问他那些电子音乐设备之类的，我们随便聊聊。我想我每天至少会这样三四次。但我根本不知道他叫什么名字。

00:43:44.100 --> 00:43:46.680
<v Jordana Gustafson>I wondered if Matt was at all surprised by this.
<v Jordana Gustafson>我想知道马特是否感到惊讶。

00:43:46.680 --> 00:44:00.430
<v Matt Ostrower>Shocked. I honestly see him between 50 and 75 times a day-- different intervals of time-- at least that, every day. Every single day.
<v Matt Ostrower>震惊。我每天真的会看到他50到75次——不同的时间间隔——至少是这样，每一天。每一天。

00:44:00.430 --> 00:44:06.030
<v Jordana Gustafson>I'm wondering if you know the name of the guy that sits right out here in the hallway?
<v Jordana Gustafson>我想知道你是否知道坐在走廊这边的那个人名字？

00:44:06.030 --> 00:44:14.750
<v Employee>Is his name-- I don't know. Works on the web, right? Kind of?
<v Employee>他是叫——我不知道。是在网上工作的吧？大概？

00:44:14.750 --> 00:44:15.870
<v Jordana Gustafson>And Matt's response?
<v Jordana Gustafson>马特的回答？

00:44:15.870 --> 00:44:19.670
<v Matt Ostrower>I'm a little surprised because I see her every day as well.
<v Matt Ostrower>我有点惊讶，因为我也每天看到她。

00:44:19.670 --> 00:44:26.090
<v Jordana Gustafson>I'm wondering if you know the name of the guy that sits in the hallway next to the printer.
<v Jordana Gustafson>我想知道你是否知道坐在打印机旁边走廊里的那个人名字。

00:44:26.090 --> 00:44:33.840
<v Employee>I don't see anybody sitting in the hallway next to the printer. I didn't think we had anybody sitting next to the printer.
<v Employee>我没看到有人坐在打印机旁走廊里。我没想到我们有人坐在打印机旁边。

00:44:33.840 --> 00:44:42.850
<v Employee>[PRINTER PRINTING]
<v Employee>[打印机打印声]

00:44:42.850 --> 00:45:07.770
<v Matt Ostrower>I've never had this kind of experience before. The whole situation is just ridiculous-- that I've been here for a year and a half, pretty much every day, and there's still people who don't know my name or what I do. And it's a little bit weird. I could go through a pretty full day without talking to anyone besides the requests from the printer. Sometimes that's it for me. [CHUCKLES]
<v Matt Ostrower>我以前从没经历过这种体验。整个情况太荒谬了——我在这里待了一年半，几乎每天都在，还是有人不知道我的名字或我是做什么的。这有点奇怪。我可以过完一整天而不跟任何人说话，除了来自打印机的请求。有时对我来说就是这样。[轻笑]

00:45:07.770 --> 00:45:32.330
<v Jordana Gustafson>Matt says the printer shows up in his dreams sometimes. In his dreams, he'll be at a party waiting in line for the bathroom, or at a parking lot at the beach, people everywhere. And there will be the printer, off to the side, chugging away, occasionally jamming.
<v Jordana Gustafson>马特说打印机有时会出现在他的梦里。在他的梦里，他在派对上排队等厕所，或者在海滩停车场，到处都是人。然后打印机就在旁边，突突地工作，偶尔卡住。

00:45:32.330 --> 00:45:39.620
<v Ira Glass>Jordana Gustafson in Boston.
<v Ira Glass>波士顿的乔达娜·古斯塔夫森。

00:45:39.620 --> 00:45:42.490
<v Ira Glass>(HOST) IRA GLASS: Act 17-- You Can't Choose Your Gift.
<v Ira Glass>第十七幕——你无法选择你的天赋。

00:45:42.490 --> 00:45:58.080
<v Richard Cary>I'm Richard Cary, and I have this one little talent. I don't know where it came from, and it fears me to think that it's something that I, myself, possess. But I'm able to make the entire sound of a swamp.
<v Richard Cary>我是理查德·卡里，我有这个小天赋。我不知道它从哪来的，想到它是属于我自己的东西，我就感到恐惧。但我能发出整个沼泽的声音。

00:45:58.080 --> 00:46:06.160
<v Richard Cary>And I will attempt to do so now. I'm not sure I'm prepared at this moment, but I'll give you the sound of a swamp.
<v Richard Cary>我现在尝试这样做。我不确定我现在准备好了，但我会给你沼泽的声音。

00:46:06.160 --> 00:46:07.600
<v Richard Cary>[CROAKING]
<v Richard Cary>[蛙鸣声]

00:46:07.600 --> 00:46:09.030
<v Richard Cary>[CRACKLING]
<v Richard Cary>[噼啪声]

00:46:09.030 --> 00:46:23.610
<v Richard Cary>[CREAKING]
<v Richard Cary>[吱吱声]

00:46:23.610 --> 00:46:29.080
<v Richard Cary>And I don't know why, but it seems to be really important at parties.
<v Richard Cary>我不知道为什么，但这在派对上似乎非常重要。

00:46:29.080 --> 00:46:31.690
<v Ira Glass>Richard Cary talked to Jim Sulzer.
<v Ira Glass>理查德·卡里和吉姆·萨尔泽聊过。

00:46:31.690 --> 00:46:35.760
<v Ira Glass>(HOST) IRA GLASS: Act 18-- Party Talk. Here's writer Chuck Klosterman.
<v Ira Glass>第十八幕——派对谈话。这里是作家查克·克洛斯特曼。

00:46:35.760 --> 00:46:54.000
<v Chuck Klosterman>This was a conversation that happened to me at a party two years ago. At one point in the conversation, I suddenly found it necessary to mention that Journey was rock's version of the TV show Dynasty. This prompted a spirited debate we then dubbed, "Monkees Equals Monkees."
<v Chuck Klosterman>这是两年前我在一个派对上发生的对话。在对话的某个点，我突然觉得有必要提到Journey乐队是摇滚界的《朝代》电视剧版本。这引发了一场我们称之为“猴子等于猴子”的激烈辩论。

00:46:54.000 --> 00:47:16.800
<v Chuck Klosterman>The goal of this game is to figure out which television show is the closest philosophical analogy to a specific rock and roll band. And the criteria are mind-blowingly complex. It's a combination of longevity, era, critical acclaim, commercial success, and, most importantly, the aesthetic soul of each artistic entity.
<v Chuck Klosterman>这个游戏的目的是找出哪部电视剧在哲学上最接近某个具体的摇滚乐队。标准复杂得让人震惊。这是寿命、时代、评论赞誉、商业成功，最重要的是，每个艺术实体的审美灵魂的结合。

00:47:16.800 --> 00:47:31.530
<v Chuck Klosterman>For example, the Rolling Stones are Gunsmoke. The Strokes-- Kiefer Sutherland's 24. Jimi Hendrix was The Twilight Zone. Devo was Fernwood 2Night.
<v Chuck Klosterman>例如，滚石乐队是《荒野大镖客》。The Strokes是基弗·萨瑟兰的《24小时》。吉米·亨德里克斯是《阴阳魔界》。Devo是《芬伍德之夜》。

00:47:31.530 --> 00:47:40.980
<v Chuck Klosterman>Lynyrd Skynyrd was The Beverly Hillbillies, which makes Molly Hatchet Petticoat Junction. The Black Crowes are That 70s Show. Hall and Oates were Bosom Buddies.
<v Chuck Klosterman>Lynyrd Skynyrd是《贝弗利山人》，这使得Molly Hatchet是《佩蒂科特交界处》。Black Crowes是《70年代秀》。Hall and Oates是《贴身伙伴》。

00:47:40.980 --> 00:47:51.870
<v Chuck Klosterman>U2 is M.A.S.H. because both kind of got preachy at the end. Dokken was Jason Bateman's short lived sitcom, It's Your Move. The Eurythmics were Mork and Mindy.
<v Chuck Klosterman>U2是《陆军野战医院》，因为两者最后都变得说教。Dokken是杰森·贝特曼的短命情景喜剧《It's Your Move》。Eurythmics是《莫克与明迪》。

00:47:51.870 --> 00:48:24.500
<v Chuck Klosterman>We even deduced comparisons for solo projects, which can only be made to series that were spawned as spin-offs. The four Beatles, post-1970, are as follows: John equals Maude, Paul equals Frasier, George equals The Jeffersons, and Ringo equals Flow. David Lee Roth's solo period after Van Halen was Knot's Landing. So there's proof-- marijuana makes you smarter.
<v Chuck Klosterman>我们甚至推断出个人项目的比较，这只能针对作为衍生剧的系列。四个披头士，1970年后，如下：约翰等于《莫德》，保罗等于《弗雷泽》，乔治等于《杰斐逊一家》，林戈等于《弗洛》。范·海伦之后的戴维·李·罗斯的个人时期是《诺茨登陆》。所以证明了——大麻会让你变得更聪明。

00:48:24.500 --> 00:48:34.790
<v Ira Glass>Chuck Klosterman reading from his book called Sex, Drugs, and Cocoa Puffs: A Low Culture Manifesto.
<v Ira Glass>查克·克洛斯特曼朗读他那本叫《性、毒品和可可泡芙：低文化宣言》。

00:48:34.790 --> 00:48:55.970
<v Ira Glass>(HOST) IRA GLASS: Act 19-- The Hard Life at the Top. Here's a ritual that takes place every summer on the last day of June-- 1,200 new army cadets, mostly teenagers, survivors of one of the most exhausting application processes in the country, arrive at West Point. And then, in the space of one morning, they're separated from their parents. Their clothes are taken away. Their hair is taken away.
<v Ira Glass>第十九幕——巅峰的艰难生活。这是每年六月最后一天举行的仪式——1200名新军校学员，大多是青少年，全国最耗尽心力的申请流程的幸存者，抵达西点军校。然后，在一个上午的时间里，他们被与父母分离。衣服被拿走。头发被剪掉。

00:48:55.970 --> 00:49:02.100
<v Ira Glass>They're weighed. They're measured. They're issued a bag, an army uniform, and underwear. They take their oath of office.
<v Ira Glass>他们被称重。被测量。他们拿到一个包、一套军装和内衣。他们进行入职宣誓。

00:49:02.100 --> 00:49:17.170
<v Ira Glass>And then, here is the first act they have as non-civilians. All they have to do is say a single sentence. Once they get it said, they can go to their barracks, but not until then. David Lipsky spent four years writing about these guys and describes what happens each year.
<v Ira Glass>然后，这是他们作为非平民的第一个举动。他们只需要说一句话。一旦说出来，他们就可以去兵营了，但在此之前不行。大卫·利普斯基花了四年时间记录这些人，描述了每年发生的事情。

00:49:17.170 --> 00:49:34.970
<v David Lipsky>The drill is simple. The new cadets have to step up to a tape line, drop their bags, and make their report to upperclassman wearing red sashes. You will walk up to the line, the red sashes tell them. And you will say, sir, new cadet Doe reports to the cadet and the red sash for the first time as ordered.
<v David Lipsky>演习很简单。新学员必须走到胶带线前，放下包，向佩戴红腰带的高年级学生汇报。你会走到线前，红腰带告诉他们。“你会说：长官，新学员多伊第一次按命令向佩戴红腰带的学员报告。”

00:49:34.970 --> 00:49:43.620
<v David Lipsky>The new cadets swallow, nod their heads, and then the screwing up begins. This cadet is so nervous, he doesn't realize he's supposed to swap his own name for Doe.
<v David Lipsky>新学员咽了口唾沫，点点头，然后搞砸就开始了。这个学员太紧张了，没意识到他应该把自己的名字换掉。

00:49:43.620 --> 00:49:45.870
<v Cadet>Sir, new cadet Doe reports to the cadet--
<v Cadet>长官，新学员多伊报告给学员——

00:49:45.870 --> 00:49:47.230
<v Red Sash>(SUBJECT) RED SASH #1: Is your last name Doe?
<v Red Sash>红腰带 #1：你的姓是多伊吗？

00:49:47.230 --> 00:49:48.080
<v Cadet>No, sir.
<v Cadet>不，长官。

00:49:48.080 --> 00:49:49.890
<v Red Sash>(SUBJECT) RED SASH #1: Then say your name, new cadet.
<v Red Sash>红腰带 #1：那就说出你的名字，新学员。

00:49:49.890 --> 00:49:50.350
<v Cadet>Yes, sir.
<v Cadet>是，长官。

00:49:50.350 --> 00:50:07.280
<v David Lipsky>"Is your last name Doe?" the red sash screams. "No, sir," says the new cadet. "Then say your name, new cadet," says the red sash. Words count, even footwork counts. New cadet Clinker, a jittery 18-year-old female cadet, finds this out when she steps a little too far forward.
<v David Lipsky>“你的姓是多伊吗？”红腰带尖叫道。“不，长官，”新学员说。“那就说出你的名字，新学员，”红腰带说。语言很重要，甚至步法也很重要。新学员克林克，一个紧张的18岁女学员，当她走得太靠前时发现了这一点。

00:50:07.280 --> 00:50:08.570
<v Cadet Clinker>Sir, new cadet Clinker.
<v Cadet Clinker>长官，新学员克林克。

00:50:08.570 --> 00:50:13.480
<v Red Sash>(SUBJECT) RED SASH #1: New cadet, look where you're standing. I told you to step up to my line. You better learn how to follow orders, new cadet. New cadet--
<v Red Sash>红腰带 #1：新学员，看看你站在哪。我让你走到我的线前。你最好学会如何听从命令，新学员。新学员——

00:50:13.480 --> 00:50:18.910
<v David Lipsky>The red sash asks her to do it again. This time, she stands in the right place, but she's forgotten what to say.
<v David Lipsky>红腰带让她再做一次。这次，她站在正确的位置，但忘记要说什么了。

00:50:18.910 --> 00:50:20.800
<v Cadet Clinker>Sir, new cadet Clinker reports to the--
<v Cadet Clinker>长官，新学员克林克报告给——

00:50:20.800 --> 00:50:22.220
<v Red Sash>(SUBJECT) RED SASH #2: I tell you to drop it back, you drop it back--
<v Red Sash>红腰带 #2：我告诉你退后，你就退后——

00:50:22.220 --> 00:50:25.980
<v Red Sash>(SUBJECT) RED SASH #1: Are you showing emotion? Did I just see a smile come across your face, new cadet?
<v Red Sash>红腰带 #1：你在表现情绪吗？我刚才看到你脸上有笑容了吗，新学员？

00:50:25.980 --> 00:50:26.360
<v Cadet Clinker>No, sir.
<v Cadet Clinker>不，长官。

00:50:26.360 --> 00:50:30.330
<v Red Sash>(SUBJECT) RED SASH #1: You are like a rock. Maintain your military discipline at all times. You understand, new cadet?
<v Red Sash>红腰带 #1：你像块石头一样。始终保持你的军事纪律。你明白吗，新学员？

00:50:30.330 --> 00:50:31.240
<v Cadet Clinker>Yes, sir.
<v Cadet Clinker>是，长官。

00:50:31.240 --> 00:50:49.810
<v David Lipsky>For these specially selected red sashes, breaking in the new cadets is a great honor. The day before, they've even practiced being hard on local civilians in a full rehearsal. Every year, a bunch of teachers, sons, daughters, groundskeepers from around town sign up for a fun day as practice cadets. Every year, a handful leave in tears.
<v David Lipsky>对于这些特别挑选的红腰带来说，打破新学员的心理防线是一种巨大的荣誉。前一天，他们甚至在全面排练中演习对当地平民表现得严厉。每年，一堆老师、儿子、女儿、园丁都会报名参加这个有趣的练习学员日。每年，都有少数人哭着离开。

00:50:49.810 --> 00:50:54.630
<v Cadet Mcleod>Sir, new cadet McLeod reporting to the cadet in the-- in the red sash for the first time as ordered.
<v Cadet Mcleod>长官，新学员麦克劳德第一次按命令向佩戴——佩戴红腰带的学员报告。

00:50:54.630 --> 00:50:56.110
<v Red Sash>Drop your salute.
<v Red Sash>放下你的敬礼。

00:50:56.110 --> 00:51:16.960
<v David Lipsky>New cadet McLeod stutters on his first attempt. He's asked to drop his salute and start over. On this one day, almost 1,200 young men and women will make their report. If everyone did it right the first time, it would take about an hour. I don't see anyone go through the first time. It takes all day. New cadet McLeod tries again, but the words won't come.
<v David Lipsky>新学员麦克劳德在第一次尝试时结巴了。他被要求放下敬礼重新开始。在这一天，将近1200名年轻男女将进行汇报。如果每个人第一次都做得对，大概需要一小时。我没看到有人一次通过。这花了一整天。新学员麦克劳德再次尝试，但词句就是出不来。

00:51:16.960 --> 00:51:20.010
<v Cadet Mcleod>Sir, n-new cadet McLeod-- new--
<v Cadet Mcleod>长官，新——新学员麦克劳德——新——

00:51:20.010 --> 00:51:23.560
<v Red Sash>Drop your salute. [INTERPOSING VOICES]
<v Red Sash>放下你的敬礼。[交叉声音]

00:51:23.560 --> 00:51:31.470
<v David Lipsky>Drop your salute, the red sash tells him. Are you a n-new cadet or a new cadet? New cadet, he says, forgetting the sir.
<v David Lipsky>放下你的敬礼，红腰带告诉他。你是“新学员”还是“新学员”？“新学员”，他说，忘记了“长官”。

00:51:31.470 --> 00:51:32.230
<v Cadet Mcleod>New cadet.
<v Cadet Mcleod>新学员。

00:51:32.230 --> 00:51:34.630
<v Red Sash>New cadet, are you going to put a 'sir' on that?
<v Red Sash>新学员，你打算加上“长官”吗？

00:51:34.630 --> 00:51:35.110
<v Cadet Mcleod>Yes, sir.
<v Cadet Mcleod>是，长官。

00:51:35.110 --> 00:51:50.890
<v David Lipsky>Poor new cadet McLeod has already screwed up twice. To get here, he spent 18 years excelling in nearly every way he can-- in schoolwork, in athletics, at student council meetings. He's beaten his way to the top of the 50,000 applicants who fill out requests for information forms.
<v David Lipsky>可怜的新学员麦克劳德已经搞砸两次了。为了到达这里，他花了18年几乎在各个方面都追求卓越——在学业上、体育上、学生会会议上。他从5万名填写信息申请表的申请者中脱颖而出。

00:51:50.890 --> 00:52:11.500
<v David Lipsky>He's been interviewed by senators, congressmen. And now, here he is, in the last place he ever thought he'd find himself, a sudden death play audition. He only has to say one line. He draws a breath, tries one more time. And after letting go of his family, his hair, his clothes, he drops the last vestige of his civilian life-- he forgets his own name.
<v David Lipsky>他接受过参议员、国会议员的采访。现在，他在这里，在他最没想到的地方，一场突然死亡的表演试镜。他只需要说一句台词。他深吸一口气，再试一次。在抛下家人、头发、衣服后，他丢掉了平民生活的最后遗迹——他忘记了自己的名字。

00:52:11.500 --> 00:52:13.460
<v Cadet Mcleod>Sir, new cadet--
<v Cadet Mcleod>长官，新学员——

00:52:13.460 --> 00:52:29.160
<v Cadet Mcleod>[INTERPOSING VOICES]
<v Cadet Mcleod>[交叉声音]

00:52:29.160 --> 00:52:39.520
<v Ira Glass>David Lipsky-- his book following one cadet class at West Point for four years is called Absolutely American.
<v Ira Glass>大卫·利普斯基——他跟随西点军校一个学员班四年的书叫《绝对美国》。

00:52:39.520 --> 00:52:56.740
<v Ira Glass>(HOST) IRA GLASS: Act 20-- The Greatest Moment I Ever Saw On a Stage. I'll say, first of all, that this moment that I saw caught me completely off guard. I was at a play where I was not expecting anything special. It was put on by an organization that works with teenagers. Storycatchers Theatre is what it's called.
<v Ira Glass>第二十幕——我在舞台上见过的最伟大的时刻。我会先说，我见到的这一刻让我完全措手不及。我当时在看一场演出，根本没预料到会有什么特别。这是一个与青少年合作的组织举办的。它叫“故事捕捉剧院”。

00:52:56.740 --> 00:53:13.680
<v Ira Glass>And among other things, they get kids who are locked up in Chicago's juvenile detention center, The Audy Home, to write and perform musicals about their lives. This one was performed by teenage girls. OK, so we're in the detention center. Folding chairs have been set up.
<v Ira Glass>除了其他事情，他们让被关在芝加哥少管所“奥迪之家”的孩子们编写并表演关于他们生活的情景剧。这一个是由少女们表演的。好吧，我们在少管所里。折叠椅已经放好了。

00:53:13.680 --> 00:53:36.330
<v Ira Glass>The girls' parents-- it's mostly mothers and grandmothers, very few men-- are sitting directly in front of the stage. And imagine for a minute what it's like to be one of those parents, OK? Your kid's locked up, possibly on very serious charges, some of these girls were. You're worried about what's going to happen to them next. You're probably still mad that they didn't listen to you in the first place and got into all this trouble and ended up behind bars.
<v Ira Glass>女孩们的父母——主要是母亲和祖母，很少有男人——直接坐在舞台前。想象一下，作为其中一个家长是什么感觉？你的孩子被关起来了，可能面临非常严重的指控，有些女孩就是。你担心接下来会发生什么。你可能还在生她们没听你的话、惹出这么多麻烦并进了监狱的气。

00:53:36.330 --> 00:53:49.000
<v Ira Glass>What can theater possibly do for you in this situation? It seems like such an old-fashioned idea that it can do anything. So there's this one scene in the play.
<v Ira Glass>在这种情况下，戏剧能为你做什么？这似乎是一个如此老派的想法，它能做任何事。所以演出中有一个场景。

00:53:49.000 --> 00:53:50.580
<v Actress 1>Where'd you get these clothes from?
<v Actress 1>你的这些衣服从哪来的？

00:53:50.580 --> 00:54:04.260
<v Ira Glass>And it's the story of this girl named Candace. And Candace basically wanted better clothes so the other kids at school wouldn't laugh at her. She steal some clothes from Nike Town. And she gets in trouble, she gets caught. And then she joins a gang to earn some money and be more popular.
<v Ira Glass>那是关于一个叫坎迪斯的女孩的故事。坎迪斯基本是因为想要更好的衣服，这样学校里的其他孩子就不会嘲笑她。她在耐克旗舰店偷了一些衣服。她惹了麻烦，被抓住了。然后她加入了一个帮派去赚钱，变得更受欢迎。

00:54:04.260 --> 00:54:12.970
<v Ira Glass>Her mom finds some drugs in the house, and a gun, and feels completely betrayed because that was not how she raised her daughter. And one thing leads to another, and Candace gets locked up.
<v Ira Glass>她妈妈在家里发现了一些毒品和一把枪，感到完全被背叛了，因为那不是她抚养女儿的方式。一件接一件事，坎迪斯被关起来了。

00:54:12.970 --> 00:54:14.630
<v Ira Glass>[MUSIC PLAYING]
<v Ira Glass>[音乐播放]

00:54:14.630 --> 00:54:31.280
<v Ira Glass>Then, the girl narrating the story says, and this is how Candace feels about her mom now. And then all the girls in the play come out on the stage and stand in a line, facing their mothers and grandmothers who are right there in front of them.
<v Ira Glass>然后，讲述故事的女孩说：“这就是坎迪斯现在对她妈妈的感觉。” 接着戏里的所有女孩走到舞台上站成一排，面对着她们就在面前的母亲和祖母。

00:54:31.280 --> 00:54:50.800
<v Singers>(SINGING) Mama, I'm sorry for what I have done. I was arrested and ended up here in the Audy Home. Oh, I'm sorry for putting you through all of this.
<v Singers>（唱歌）妈妈，我对不起我所做的一切。我被捕了，最终关进了奥迪之家。哦，对不起让你经历了这一切。

00:54:50.800 --> 00:55:09.150
<v Singers>I know you are mad about the good times that we missed. I'm ready to come back home. I'm willing to make a change.
<v Singers>我知道你因为我们错过的美好时光而生气。我准备好回家了。我愿意做出改变。

00:55:09.150 --> 00:55:25.940
<v Ira Glass>One of the verses goes, "Mama, I'm sorry for making you come to court, for almost losing your job to give me moral support. Mama, I'm sorry for putting your through all this stress, for making you worry yourself and depressed. I'm ready to come back home. I'm willing to make a change."
<v Ira Glass>其中一节歌词是，“妈妈，对不起让你来法庭，对不起差点让你丢了工作来给我精神支持。妈妈，对不起让你承受这一切压力，让你担心自己并感到抑郁。我准备好回家了。我愿意做出改变。”

00:55:25.940 --> 00:55:45.530
<v Ira Glass>And, by this time, the girls are all crying, the parents are all crying. And each girl has a cut-out little heart, like on Valentine's Day, like that, made from red construction paper, the size of your palm. And written on each one is the words, "I'm sorry."
<v Ira Glass>到了这个时候，女孩们都在哭，家长们都在哭。每个女孩手里都有一个红色的建筑纸做的小切纸心，像情人节那种，手掌大小。每个上面都写着，“对不起”。

00:55:45.530 --> 00:55:56.070
<v Ira Glass>And each girl goes out into the audience to where her mom is sitting, or her grandma is sitting, and hands her the heart. And the parents are crying, and the kids are crying, and everybody is hugging. It was really something.
<v Ira Glass>每个女孩走向观众席妈妈坐的地方，或者祖母坐的地方，把心递给她。家长们在哭，孩子们在哭，每个人都在拥抱。那真是了不起。

00:55:56.070 --> 00:56:15.670
<v Singers>(SINGING) --make a change. I'll do anything for you, mom. Please forget about yesterday. Mama, I'm sorry. [SNIFFLES]
<v Singers>（唱歌）——做出改变。妈，我什么都愿意为你做。请忘记昨天。妈妈，对不起。[抽泣]

00:56:15.670 --> 00:56:18.810
<v Singers>I'm sorry. I'm sorry. Mama, I'm sorry.
<v Singers>对不起。对不起。妈妈，对不起。

00:56:18.810 --> 00:56:37.400
<v Ira Glass>Here they were-- not just saying this to their mothers, but saying it publicly in front of the world, in front of their friends. Saying this thing that could be so hard to say in any case. Singing it out and hoping that it can heal something that is going to be hard to heal, no matter what you do.
<v Ira Glass>她们在这里——不仅是对妈妈说这些，而且在世界面前、在朋友面前公开说。说这件在任何情况下都很难说出口的事。唱出来，并希望它能治愈一些无论你做什么都难以治愈的东西。

00:56:37.400 --> 00:56:51.400
<v Singers>(SINGING) Mama, I'm sorry. [SNIFFLES] I'm sorry. I'm sorry. I'm sorry. I'm sorry. Mama, I'm sorry.
<v Singers>（唱歌）妈妈，对不起。[抽泣] 对不起。对不起。对不起。对不起。妈妈，对不起。

00:56:51.400 --> 00:57:00.560
<v Ira Glass>And that's our show for today, 20 Acts in 60 Minutes. Thanks today to Atlantic Public Media, WCAI and WNAN, our website, thisamericanlife.org.
<v Ira Glass>这就是我们今天的节目，60分钟内20幕。感谢大西洋公共媒体、WCAI和WNAN，我们的网站，thisamericanlife.org。

00:57:00.560 --> 00:57:11.300
<v Ira Glass>This American Life is delivered to public radio stations by PRX, the Public Radio Exchange. Thanks, as always, to our program's co-founder, Mr. Tony Malatia, who remembers all the way back to when we started the show.
<v Ira Glass>《美国生活》由公共广播交易所PRX发送给公共广播电台。一如既往地感谢我们节目的联合创始人托尼·马拉蒂亚先生，他记得我们开始这个节目以来的一切。

00:57:11.300 --> 00:57:17.480
<v Valion Lotze>I think it was right around the time we had this big fight about gumballs, which I'm not going to get into because it's pretty embarrassing.
<v Valion Lotze>我记得那正好是我们为口香糖大吵一架的时候，我不打算细说，因为挺尴尬的。

00:57:17.480 --> 00:57:22.480
<v Ira Glass>I'm Ira Glass, back next week with more stories of This American Life.
<v Ira Glass>我是艾拉·格拉斯，下周回来，带来更多《美国生活》的故事。
